- [ ] every user-facing feature the history records is named in the README, grouped as a reader wants them
- [ ] nothing the README claims is stale - each claim checked against the current build
- [ ] the survey''s source list (the commits or ranges each section came from) is recorded on this ticket
- [ ] yarn validate green', NULL, NULL, NULL, 4500, '2026-10-25 05:56:30.556803+00', '2026-10-25 17:59:25.158591+00', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, 4, NULL);
INSERT INTO public.issue (id, project_id, sequence_number, type_id, column_id, title, description, priority, start_date, due_date, "position", created_at, updated_at, assignee_user_id, delegate_user_id, initiative_id, archived_at, share_token, parent_id, share_extended, cycle_id, estimate_points, estimate_minutes) VALUES (381, 1, 381, 3, 4, 'The Check for updates row: one check per launch, download and refresh glyphs, and the green up-to-date tick', 'Goal: rework the settings sheet''s Check for updates row - its place in the Other card, a once-per-launch guard on the check, and its checking and result presentation - so a press reads as download-then-refresh and settles on a green tick. The follow-up to T-376''s landed row, on the owner''s spec (2026-10-27).

Where: components/sheets/screens/Settings.tsx (the Other card''s row order and the update row''s states), device/updates.ts (the manual verdict it already returns), shared/i18n/* (the six catalogs), the row''s suite.

Acceptance:
- [ ] The Other card reads Language, Help, What''s New, Check for updates, in that order: the What''s New and Help rows swap their current places, Check for updates stays last.
- [ ] A manual check runs at most once per opening of the settings sheet: after one press the row settles into its result state and no further press starts another check until the sheet closes and opens again, which re-arms it and rests the row (an unpersisted in-memory flag, not storage; an answer whose sheet closed mid-flight lands nowhere). Owner order 2026-10-27, superseding the launch-scoped guard.
- [ ] The row''s left glyph becomes a down-arrow (a download arrow), replacing the current check glyph.
- [ ] The row''s right side carries two arrows forming a circle, each pointing at the other''s tail (the classic two-arrow refresh glyph), where rows otherwise carry a chevron.
- [ ] While the check runs, the two arrows spin clockwise and the label reads "Checking for updates..." in the pressed locale, with the trailing dots as part of the checking state.
- [ ] On the up-to-date verdict, the two circular arrows disappear, the left glyph becomes a green tick (green replacing the purple for the settled state), and the label reads the existing up-to-date string.
- [ ] On the newer-version verdict, the sheet closes and the update modal presents (the launch check''s own modal on iOS, the store flow on Android) - the landed T-376 behavior, kept as is.
- [ ] The row mirrors correctly in Arabic: icons and layout flip with the direction, and the checking label carries its own Arabic string (with the other four locales'' equivalents: ar, so, hi, ms, th).
- [ ] The catalog lock re-hashes for any changed English source; the row''s suite pins the once-per-launch guard, the three visual states, and the row order.

Owner quotes that are the spec: the spin is "clockwise, round and round"; the up-to-date change is "purple to a green colour and then a tick instead, just to show the user, hey, you''re up to date, nothing to do"; the guard is "they can click that button one time only... when they close the app, that''s when it resets back to zero... they can''t just background it either". The owner ruled this ticket is planned and grilled heavily before execution, and executed by a dispatched lane.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 16500, '2026-10-27 04:41:57.167332+00', '2026-10-27 10:13:54.832431+00', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, 7, NULL);
INSERT INTO public.issue (id, project_id, sequence_number, type_id, column_id, title, description, priority, start_date, due_date, "position", created_at, updated_at, assignee_user_id, delegate_user_id, initiative_id, archived_at, share_token, parent_id, share_extended, cycle_id, estimate_points, estimate_minutes) VALUES (403, 1, 403, NULL, 1, 'The update modal presents at every launch, even with connectivity off', 'Goal: the launch update modal presents only when a fresh, successful check finds the store ahead of the installed build - never from stale state, never with the network unreachable.

Where: app/index.tsx''s launch check (the 1500ms deferred checkForUpdates feeding setPopupUpdateEnabled), device/updates.ts (getStoreVersion''s fetch and its Cache-Control: no-cache against NSURLSession''s disk cache, the 24-hour stamp), stores/ui.ts (popupUpdateLastCheckAtom''s persistence across relaunches), stores/version.ts (getInstalledVersion from Constants.expoConfig).

How it was found (owner, 2026-10-27, on the XS carrying the 2.11.27-era build): with Wi-Fi off, opening the app presents "Update available" before anything else; Later dismisses it; the manual Check for updates row correctly fails ("Failed to check. Try again") offline and greens ("You''re up to date") online; the store page reads 2.4.39 while the installed build reads far ahead of it; and the modal returns on EVERY relaunch, connectivity irrelevant. Tapping Update opens the store as built.

The contradiction that names the investigation''s first step: within one process, the manual comparison read the store BEHIND the build (green) while the launch check read it AHEAD (modal) - unreachable from the same inputs in the current code, where a modal needs isNewerVersion(installed, store) true from a successful fetch and the throttle stamps every success. So first establish what the launch check actually saw: the installed binary''s real version off the phone, the launch check''s store answer (its log lines), and whether an offline launch serves the iTunes lookup from NSURLSession''s disk cache despite no-cache - then the throttle''s stamp persistence across relaunches. Any theory must explain modal-every-launch AND green-manual-offline-fails in the same session.

Acceptance:
- [ ] The launch check''s actual inputs and answer are read off the phone (log lines or a debug read): the installed version, the store version it compared against, and the fetch''s fate with connectivity off
- [ ] The root cause is named with evidence and fixed: with connectivity off, a launch presents no modal; the modal presents only when a fresh successful comparison finds the store ahead
- [ ] The 24-hour throttle holds across relaunches: a second launch inside the window runs no check and presents nothing
- [ ] The manual row''s behavior (T-396''s failure and verdict states) is unchanged, pinned by its suite
- [ ] Full unit coverage of every changed line, red before green; read once on glass on the XS', NULL, NULL, NULL, 20500, '2026-10-27 23:16:34.258255+00', '2026-10-27 23:16:34.364755+00', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-10-28 00:56:00.667423+00', NULL, NULL, false, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO public.issue (id, project_id, sequence_number, type_id, column_id, title, description, priority, start_date, due_date, "position", created_at, updated_at, assignee_user_id, delegate_user_id, initiative_id, archived_at, share_token, parent_id, share_extended, cycle_id, estimate_points, estimate_minutes) VALUES (429, 1, 429, 3, 4, 'Fifty languages, wave three of three: the Europe, Central Asia and India eight (es, nl, kk, ky, tg, tk, wo, mr)', 'Goal: take the app from twenty-six locale catalogs to thirty-four by shipping this wave''s eight languages, the third of three parallel waves that together add twenty-four and reach fifty. Code only: no phone, no simulator, no screenshot, no build, no install. The owner reads the result afterwards and rules.

Languages: es (Spanish), nl (Dutch), kk (Kazakh), ky (Kyrgyz), tg (Tajik), tk (Turkmen), wo (Wolof), mr (Marathi).
Font note: kk, ky, tg and tk ride Roboto''s Cyrillic, mr rides the bundled Noto Sans Devanagari pair, and the rest are Latin, so this wave bundles no new font; the plan''s decision 6 records the fontTools measurement, by explicit codepoint in both weights, that proves it.

Where: the eight catalogs under shared/i18n/ beside the twenty-six shipped; the sourcing record shared/i18n/sourced-names.json; shared/typography.ts and shared/languageNames.ts; shared/i18n/locale.ts, loader.ts, native-strings.json, widget-statics.json and catalog-lock.json with scripts/catalog-lock.mjs; app.json''s expo.locales and CFBundleLocalizations; widgets/PrayerWidget.tsx and widgets/LockPrayerWidget.tsx; the gates under shared/__tests__/; README.md. The recipe is the shipped waves #410 and #419 and their plan docs (Docs pages 48 and 49).

Acceptance:
- [x] Eight catalogs ship at en''s parity (205 keys) and satisfy the naming rule, with sourcing recorded per language so a jq census over shared/i18n/sourced-names.json prints zero rows with null text, source or confidence across the eight''s names and explanations.
- [x] The eight ids sit in LOCALE_IDS, the loader, the picker''s endonyms, native-strings.json, widget-statics.json, app.json''s CFBundleLocalizations and expo.locales, the four widget STATIC tables and their locale-finder lists, the detection rows and the gates; the T429_EIGHT sourcing row is green in catalogGates.
- [x] typography.ts maps es, nl, kk, ky, tg, tk and wo to the LATIN record and mr to the bundled Devanagari pair, and typography.test.ts covers both.
- [x] yarn validate exits 0 at full coverage with en''s catalog-lock hash unchanged, and the README''s Features bullet names every shipped language with a count equal to LOCALE_IDS.length at the merged commit.
- [x] The merge protocol holds: every shared list takes this wave''s rows appended at its end, and after a rebase onto uat the additive resolution keeps both waves'' rows with the README count at the merged length, never this wave''s number alone.
- [x] No device work of any kind: no build, no install, no simulator, no screenshot. The wave''s proof is the suite alone.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 23500, '2026-10-28 23:19:35.346098+00', '2026-10-29 00:29:55.180997+00', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, false, NULL, 20, NULL);
