INSERT INTO public.issue_activity (id, issue_id, kind, actor_name, body, action, created_at, actor_user_id, initiative_id, reply_to_id, payload, edited_at) VALUES (3294, 327, 'comment', 'owner', 'Step 4 done: the interleaved A/B/A dissolves the Step 3 attribution. No commit inside 887d7949..1c22014c owns the regression; the entrance-snap component is not a property of any build, and the one quantity that is stable - the tap-to-first-draw dead zone - is version-invariant across every build ever measured on this phone.

The stamp interrogation (the window''s only runtime-visible change). WHATS_NEW.version is the banner constant, never rendered and never a gate: shouldShowWhatsNew reads item stamps alone, the archive''s newest stamp is 2.0.37 in both builds, and no item was stamped in 1c22014c - so the What''s New modal cannot pop on a non-first launch of either build, and it popped on none tonight. The versionName change reaches exactly one consumer, handleAppUpgrade, captured live on the discarded launches: the 2.4.24-over-2.4.23 install logs "Upgrade detected" plus "Reset notification schedule timestamp to force reschedule", the reverse install logs "Downgrade detected (skipping cache clear)", and every measured launch logs same-version and does no upgrade work. The discarded first launch absorbs all of it. There is no path from this commit into any measured animation.

The A/B/A (2.4.24, then 2.4.23, then 2.4.24 again; the reverse of Step 3''s order, so the clean candidate measured last on the warmest device). Controls held throughout: battery 47 to 66 percent on USB charge (recorded with every run, never below Step 3''s clean-anchor readings), battery saver off, no screensharing agent, the second Athan-family package force-stopped before every flow, first post-install launch discarded each time, force-stop between flows, aapt2 badging asserted per install, User 0 alone, and deterministic rebuilds (both 2.4.24 builds byte-identical bundles, 2.4.23 likewise, differing from each other exactly as the diff predicts).

Marks never moved: overlay_open 134-151 and overlay_close 139-156 (A1 142/148, B 151/156, A2 134/139), sheet_alert_open 429-456, sheet_settings_open 372-385, closes 204-210 - all inside the cross-build bands Steps 1 and 3 already recorded. The verdict was in the frames, and the frames flip with the run, not the build: the settings entrance snapped on 2.4.24 first-run-cool (first drawn frame 80.4 percent traveled) and on 2.4.23 deep-load (80 percent), then rendered clean from 24.1 percent on 2.4.24 again minutes later; the alert entrance drew clean from 24 percent on both A and B, then opened at 43 percent behind a 0.9s dead zone on the second A run. The overlay entrances rendered intermediate states everywhere (3-5 veil opacity steps, first drawn at 10-12 percent of travel) with tap-to-first-draw dead zones of 0.36-0.92s spread evenly across both builds and every thermal position. Step 3''s clean 2.4.23 versus snapped 2.4.24 split did not reproduce in either direction: the same build snaps and renders cleanly within one night under fixed controls.

Verdict, honestly: the battery confound is exonerated within the range covered (failures at 51-63 percent tonight against Step 3''s clean readings at 22-27; no correlation, though tonight never sampled below 47). The regression signature that remains - a version-invariant tap-adjacent dead zone plus entrance first-draws that land anywhere from 24 to 88 percent of travel run-to-run - is not owned by this window and cannot be bisected by anchor builds as the plan assumed, because a build does not have one signature. The vision reads also flag that part of the snap is capture loss: the settings snap''s missing early frames match the same entrance''s own later curve on both builds, and the 2.4.11 anchor''s nine-alpha close reads four alphas tonight on both builds. The surviving per-run variables are the mock''s per-launch reseed (tonight''s seeds put next-prayer boundaries seconds away; the vision caught the overlay auto-closing on one, the selection pill sliding between rows at another) and the SD820 encoder dropping frames under load. Step 5 should either widen the window past 2.4.11 or re-frame around those two variables before naming a code cause.

Instrument findings for the record: maestro 2.10.0 no longer substitutes ${APP_ID} from the environment, so the committed flows driven through baseline-compare.sh launch no app, measure the launcher, and can drive the other Athan package through the dock icon - the sheets marks ran through a session-temp mirror with --env and an assertVisible launch gate instead (the committed script stayed untouched). The stock pre-tap close audit is currently unusable: the overlay auto-closes inside its 3s pre-wait whenever the mock''s seeded boundary is near, so eight consecutive stock close recordings captured re-opens; the valid close evidence comes from a 1s-pre-wait manual variant of the same instrument, and A1''s two overlay recordings were superseded by A2''s after a day roll contaminated them. Tap points re-verified from the live tree each build (row and bell at y~1012, settings 539,1833). Evidence dirs, worktree-local and untracked: 2.4.24 run overlay-open-014125 (roll-contaminated), sheet-alert-open-014207, sheet-settings-open-014229; 2.4.23 run overlay-open-020349, sheet-alert-open-020431, sheet-settings-open-020454, overlay-close-manual-024740; 2.4.24 recheck overlay-open-023620, sheet-alert-open-023641, sheet-settings-open-023705, overlay-close-manual-023544. No repository changes were made; the lane branch stands at 2.4.25 (4ffd1f34). The 3T now carries the 2.4.23 anchor build at User 0, battery 66 percent on charge.', NULL, '2026-10-25 03:02:17.478408+00', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', NULL, NULL, '{}', NULL);
