INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (20, 1, NULL, 'T-116', '# T-116

## Goal

Apple requires iPhone Duo readiness from April 2027. This row''s earlier life proved the existing adaptive layout renders the Duo with zero Duo code on iOS 27.0. What is wrong today: the requirement is named only in the memory of that close, and current uat (2.1.19) is unverified on the iOS 27.1 Duo simulator. True when done: Apple''s iOS 27 / SDK 58 Duo requirements are named from Apple''s own skills and docs with every hard requirement beyond running recorded; the app at current uat shows one centred schedule in each pose on the iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1); one Duo screenshot is attached to this row; the branch carries zero Duo-specific layout code; every other form factor is unchanged.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-19 The research base is four official artifacts: Apple''s news post of 2026-10-05 (developer.apple.com/news/?id=kkphp5qo), the prepare page (developer.apple.com/iphone-duo/prepare/), Tech Talks 111461 and 111463, and the App Resizability skill exported from this machine''s Xcode 27.1 with `xcrun agent skills export`. Why: the ticket names official skills and docs; these are Apple''s own words, and the export works here (Xcode 27.1, Build 27A9275, verified 2026-10-19).
- 2026-10-19 The one hard requirement beyond running: from April 2027 any app or game submitted needs iPhone Duo screenshots in App Store Connect (news post, closing paragraph). Everything else in the material is optimization guidance phrased can, consider, should. Why: that sentence is the only imperative with a deadline in Apple''s Duo material.
- 2026-10-19 Zero Duo code stands. No official source mandates a Duo-specific layout; Apple''s own description of a non-adapting app on the open Duo is a window centred in the display with empty space around it, which is the owner''s sanctioned rendering. The portrait lock holds: "iPhone Duo respects your supported interface orientations, but your app will scale on the inner display" and "iPhone Duo will continue to honor the UIRequiresFullScreen key" (Tech Talk 111461).
- 2026-10-19 The attached screenshot is the open pose. Why: it is the one image that shows the claim under verification, the centred schedule in the portrait window on the inner display; the closed pose renders like any iPhone.
- 2026-10-19 The device step builds locally from the branch with \`npx expo run:ios -d <SIMULATOR_UDID> --configuration Release\` and targets the Duo by udid (committed text uses the SIMULATOR_UDID placeholder). Why: two simulators are booted (an iPhone 18 Pro on 27.0 beside the Duo on 27.1), so a bare destination hits the wrong one; the branch differs from uat only in version files and board records, so the built app is uat''s code. A local build serves the mock API; the verification reads layout, not times, so the mock is not a confound.
- 2026-10-19 Apple''s three pattern families are answered by reads recorded on this row, not by code: `UIScreen` has zero hits in ios/ outside Pods; every JS geometry consumer reads the live window through hooks/useWindowDimensions.ts (RN''s live hook; the launch-memoized version was the exact anti-pattern Apple names and its own history note records the fix); no userInterfaceIdiom, interfaceOrientation, statusBarOrientation, isPad or isTablet layout branch exists in the app''s source (greps run 2026-10-19, zero hits). Why: the patterns are absent, so the skill''s scan has nothing to force and no change follows.

## Files

Read:
- app/Screen.tsx and shared/constants.ts (SIZE.contentMaxWidth = 500): the centred adaptive layout under verification.
- hooks/useWindowDimensions.ts: the live window-size hook every geometry consumer uses.
- app.json (orientation portrait) and plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.js: the orientation lock Apple honors.
- e2e/README.md: the Gotchas, read before any measurement.
- The App Resizability skill exported to a scratch directory outside the repository.
- Apple''s artifacts named in Decisions, fetched and cited by URL.

Changed:
- package.json and app.json: the version bump, patch, on every step commit.
- board/snapshot/board.sql: the row''s records regenerated per step commit.
- android/app/build.gradle was never written: this lane''s worktree prebuilt ios/ only and holds no android tree, and no build.gradle anywhere carries the lane''s versions. The three-file rule''s third leg rides app.json into the next prebuild, which derives versionName from it, so no artifact is wrong; in git the branch diff names exactly package.json, app.json and board/snapshot/board.sql. (Audit repair 2026-10-20: this line previously claimed an on-disk bump that no tree received.)
- No code file changes. A step that seems to need one is a stop, not an edit.

## Design

The deliverable is an evidence record, not a change. The app''s answer to the Duo is built: one schedule, centred, capped at 500pt through Screen.tsx''s maxWidth and alignSelf center, riding the live window size. Apple''s material carries the three things this row names: apps run on Duo whatever their SDK; a portrait-locked app scales into a window on the inner display rather than rotating; the only dated obligation is App Store screenshots from April 2027. The verification re-runs the prior close''s protocol on iOS 27.1 at current uat: both poses, both schedules, the fold transition.

Alternatives rejected:
- Any Duo-branched layout, a second schedule, a centre settings button, a centre-anchored sheet: the owner rejected these twice (2026-10-18, 2026-10-19) and no Apple requirement forces them; Apple''s default for non-adapting apps is the centred window this app already renders.
- Landscape support to fill the open panel: it turns rotation on for every iPhone and iPad; the owner excluded it at the prior close and nothing in Apple''s material makes it an obligation.
- Findings recorded in a repository MD file: the records rule keeps the row''s records on the board (this doc, the ticket''s comments, the snapshot), and code is the documentation.

## Steps

1. Step 1: Apple''s requirements named and recorded (docs, 1 point). Export the App Resizability skill to a scratch directory outside the repository, read it, fetch the four official artifacts, re-run the three pattern reads, then write the Requirements section into this doc: each requirement, its source URL or export path, and the one hard requirement beyond running quoted verbatim. One commit: version bump plus the snapshot carrying this doc''s new section.
2. Step 2: both poses verified on the iPhone Duo simulator (device, 3 points). Build from the branch with \`npx expo run:ios -d <SIMULATOR_UDID> --configuration Release\` (Release makes the installed app self-contained, so pose driving never depends on a live Metro), then: closed pose, one schedule filling the outer display; open pose, one schedule in the centred portrait window; both schedules reachable; the fold transition keeps the app alive and the overlay reachable; one open-pose screenshot attached to this row from scratch outside the worktree; every reading transcribed into the ticket comment. One commit: version bump plus the snapshot. This step runs alone in a fresh executor session.
3. Step 3: the row''s records closed (docs, 1 point). Read the branch diff and record that it names no layout file, so every other form factor is unchanged by construction; tick the subtasks; regenerate the snapshot. One commit: version bump plus the snapshot.

## Risks

- iOS 27.1 grants a window materially different from the prior close''s 469x669, or the 27.1 SDK''s full-display behavior pulls the rendering away from the owner''s sanctioned centred schedule. What shows it: the open-pose reading. If the schedule is not centred, is clipped, or splits: stop and table it for the owner; no code is written to chase it.
- The skill export scans Xcode-agent conventions and names nothing applicable to a React Native tree. Acceptable: the prepare page''s own pattern list is the authority, the reads answer it, and the export attempt and its output are recorded.
- The Duo simulator reboots or another session takes it. The step re-reads the simctl device list first and drives poses through the agent-device fold command.
- A screenshot or recording lands in the worktree. The screenshot is written to scratch outside the worktree and attached from there; git status before every commit shows no image file.

## Proof

- Requirements named: this doc''s Requirements section carries each requirement with its source URL or the skill export path, and the one hard requirement beyond running quoted verbatim.
- Both poses verified: the Step 2 ticket comment transcribes the closed-pose and open-pose readings (window grant, schedule centring, both schedules, fold liveness) taken on the iPhone Duo simulator at iOS 27.1.
- Screenshot attached, never committed: view_issue_images on this row shows it; `git diff --stat uat..lane/116` lists no image path.
- Zero Duo code: `git diff --name-only uat..lane/116` lists exactly package.json, app.json and board/snapshot/board.sql; the android/ and ios/ trees are gitignored and untracked, so neither can appear in a diff. (Audit repair 2026-10-20: this line previously named android/app/build.gradle, which a diff can never list.)
- Every other form factor unchanged: the same diff read names no layout file, and `yarn validate` is green at every step commit.


## Requirements

Sources read 2026-10-19: the news post (developer.apple.com/news/?id=kkphp5qo), the prepare page (developer.apple.com/iphone-duo/prepare/), Tech Talks 111461 and 111463 (developer.apple.com/videos/play/tech-talks/111461/ and .../111463/), and the App Resizability skill exported from this machine''s Xcode 27.1 (Build 27A9275) into a scratch directory outside the repository with `xcrun agent skills export --output-dir <scratch>/duo-skills`: app-resizability/SKILL.md and references/uiscreen-task.md, orientation-task.md, idiom-task.md, safe-area-task.md, scene-lifecycle-task.md.

### The one hard requirement beyond running

News post, closing paragraph, verbatim: "Starting April 2027, any apps or games submitted will need to include screenshots for iPhone Duo." It is the only dated imperative in Apple''s Duo material. Everything else below is guidance phrased can, consider, or should.

### The statements that sanction this app''s shape

Verbatim, Tech Talks 111461:

- "iPhone Duo respects your supported interface orientations, but your app will scale on the inner display, including in Split View multitasking."
- "iPhone Duo will continue to honor the UIRequiresFullScreen key, but your app will still resize when someone opens or closes their iPhone Duo."

Prepare page, the iOS 26 SDK paragraph, on a non-adapting app on the open Duo: "your app will appear in the center of the display, with empty space surrounding the window". A centred window is Apple''s own rendering for an app that does not adapt, which is the owner''s sanctioned shape.

### The SDK ladder (prepare page, Before you begin)

- iOS 26 SDK or earlier: runs on the Duo, does not adapt; open pose shows the centred window with empty space around it.
- iOS 27 SDK: resizes to fill most of the inner display, still avoiding the status bar on the right edge.
- iOS 27.1 SDK or later: optimized; extends to the full display with toolbars and tab bars laying out vertically below the status bar, and content must adapt to the bars becoming vertical before shipping.

The local build uses Xcode 27.1 (SDK 58). The portrait lock means the inner display renders the scaled window the Tech Talk names, not the full-display layout, so the vertical-bars caveat describes a shape this app does not take.

### Requirements and guidance that bear on this app

- Every app runs on iPhone Duo whatever its SDK (prepare page; Tech Talks 111461: "Your app will work on iPhone Duo even if you haven''t built it with the iOS 27 SDK"). Recompiling with Xcode 27.1 adds development support (news post); the local build already runs Xcode 27.1.
- The skill''s Prerequisites (app-resizability/SKILL.md), read against the generated Info.plist at the same commit: launch screen satisfied (UILaunchStoryboardName present; absent means the ITMS-90870 upload rejection, TN3208); scene lifecycle satisfied (UIApplicationSceneManifest plus SceneDelegate.swift in the Expo-generated shell; scene-lifecycle-task.md records scene lifecycle as required of iOS 27 SDK builds); the portrait-only declarations for iPhone and iPad and UIRequiresFullScreen=true are exactly the lock the Tech Talk says the Duo honors. SKILL.md, writing about iOS 27 generally, says the platform ignores UIRequiresFullScreen and resizes the scene anyway (TN3192), and its rule is to change no key unilaterally; the Duo-specific statement governs the Duo. No key changes.
- The skill''s five scan tasks (UIScreen.main, orientation-based layout, idiom checks, symmetric safe-area assumptions, application lifecycle) cover the three pattern families the prepare page''s Step 2 names for manual review. The reads below answer each family against this tree.
- Tech Talks 111463 is optimization guidance throughout: displacement patterns, reserved regions (division for the fold, occlusion for the camera), ArrangementView. Its close is phrased "Audit your app''s centered layouts and consider whether a two-column layout or a displacement pattern fits". Nothing there obliges the two-schedule or per-panel layouts the owner rejected.
- Store surface, not code (news post): updated screenshot and app preview specifications, the App Store Connect preview tool for Duo assets, and a product-page badge when Duo screenshots are submitted. The April 2027 screenshot requirement lands here.

### The three pattern reads, 2026-10-19 at 2.1.19 (commit 2d6e7631)

(a) `grep -rn "UIScreen" ios --include="*.swift" --include="*.m"` excluding Pods: zero hits. This worktree carries no ios/ tree (gitignored, not prebuilt here), so the read ran against the generated tree at the same commit in the main checkout: 18 Swift files outside Pods (the Athan shell, SceneDelegate, the widget targets), zero hits for UIScreen case-insensitive with headers included.

(b) Direct `Dimensions.get(` across app/ components/ hooks/ shared/ stores/ device/ modules/ plugins/ widgets/: zero in production code. The three hits are test files asserting against the mocked window (components/sheets/screens/__tests__/Qibla.test.tsx, components/overlay/__tests__/VeilBackdrop.test.tsx, components/overlay/__tests__/Overlay.test.tsx). Every production geometry consumer imports hooks/useWindowDimensions.ts, one line re-exporting RN''s live hook, whose in-file history note records that the launch-memoized version was removed because it froze absolute-geometry consumers on resizable windows. That memoized version was the prepare page''s named anti-pattern ("Reading the size once at launch or in `viewIsAppearing` isn''t enough, because a fold can resize your app in the middle of a session"), already fixed.

(c) `grep -rniE "userInterfaceIdiom|interfaceOrientation|statusBarOrientation|isPad|isTablet"` over the same dirs: zero layout branches. The only hits are the declaration strings in plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.js (UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait, UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown) writing UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad: the supported-orientations lock the Tech Talk says the Duo respects, not a branch reading current orientation or device class.

Zero hits across all three families means the skill''s scan has nothing to find, and zero Duo-specific code follows.

## Verification (Step 2)

Recorded 2026-10-19 on the iPhone Duo simulator, iOS 27.1 (SIMULATOR_UDID). Local Release build of the branch tip (2.1.20 read off the installed bundle, CFBundleShortVersionString), mock API, layout only. Same process (pid 22893) across the whole session.

- Closed pose: outer display 466x678; the app window fills it edge to edge ([0,0 466x678]). One schedule (one pager cell), countdown and day header present, rows 442pt at x=12: 12pt margins each side. Both schedules reachable (Standard and Extras). Settings sheet opens full panel width ([0,140.7 466x537.3]); its Help row opens the Help modal (eight topics plus Close, content 438pt with 14pt margins).
- Open pose: inner display 951x669; the app rides a portrait window 469x669, full height, x 241..709: 241pt empty each side, centred (measured from the capture''s content extent). The system status bar sits on the panel''s right edge (x ~850-923), outside the window. One schedule in the window, rows 445pt at x=12 (12pt in-window margins), countdown and day header present. Both schedules reachable; settings sheet full window width ([0,139.35 469x529.65]); Help row opens the same modal (topics 441pt, 14pt margins).
- Fold liveness: with the focused-prayer overlay on (Magrib), open to closed to open kept the process alive (pid unchanged); the overlay and its close-catchers survived both folds and re-laid per panel. No relaunch.
- iOS 27.1 instrument note: the fold command''s hinge-angle verification read returns no sample on this simulator (three attempts), so each pose was confirmed by the lit panel''s viewport and the capture''s dimensions instead. The numbers match the prior close: 466x678 closed, 469x669 centred in 951x669 open. The centred window is the owner''s sanctioned rendering: no split, no clip.
- Screenshot: duo-open-pose.png attached to row 116 (attachment f48c5d44-b5be-4106-a343-faa1a1c104e9), open pose, the centred-schedule claim; written to scratch outside the worktree and never committed.

## Records close (Step 3)

Recorded 2026-10-19 at 2.1.21 (HEAD 0dd682b7), before this step''s commit. `git diff --name-only uat..HEAD`, with uat fetched and confirmed at 2.1.19 (2d6e7631, equal to origin/uat), names exactly three files: package.json, app.json and board/snapshot/board.sql. No layout file appears. The android/ and ios/ trees are gitignored and untracked, so they cannot appear in any diff; the android/app/build.gradle version bump lives on disk only, outside git. The three named files are the version bump and the board''s records: zero code files changed, so nothing any other form factor renders can have moved, and every other form factor is unchanged by construction. Zero Duo-specific layout code ships, and the acceptance line''s escape hatch is unused: no requirement in the Requirements section above forces Duo code. The one hard requirement beyond running is the App Store Duo screenshot from April 2027, a submission obligation, not a layout obligation.', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, NULL, 19456, 6, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-19 22:14:30.645252+00', '2026-10-19 23:02:43.915816+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (21, 1, NULL, 'T-264', '# T-264

## Goal

What is wrong today: on the iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1), open and half-open poses, the app renders inside a system-scaled centred 469x669 portrait window on the 951x669 inner display. The window delivers a phantom top safe-area inset of about 80pt, which app/Screen.tsx honors, so the countdown block sits about 82pt lower than the closed pose and the Standard page''s Isha row overlaps the Settings button by about 46pt. True when done: the phantom is not honored, the countdown sits at its designed offset (the closed-pose class), Isha clears the Settings button, the black bars remain untouched and are recorded on this row as Apple''s own letterbox around a portrait-locked app, and portrait poses and every phone geometry render bit-identically to today.

THE RULING (lead, agent-ruled 2026-10-20, row comment 2371): Vehicle C. Keep Apple''s sanctioned scaled window; fix the phantom gap and the overlap inside it; the bars stay. Vehicles A (landscape in the iPhone orientation array, every iPhone may rotate) and B (a Duo-scoped orientation gate) stay tabled on that comment and run the day the owner orders one. The earlier edge-to-edge acceptance is revised accordingly.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-20 The mechanisms, measured on the booted iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1, SIMULATOR_UDID) driving the installed 2.1.20 Release build (code equal to uat modulo version files) before any change was designed. Bars: a panel-level screenshot (951x669) reads pure black (0,0,0) from x=0 to about x=241 and from about x=709 to x=951, and the app gradient (#031a4c to #5b1eaa) spans exactly between; the app tree spans 469x669. The bars are the system letterbox outside the app''s window; no app view or color reaches them (the darkest app color is #031a4c = (3,26,76)). Gap and push-down: the countdown''s prayer-name text starts at y=95 in the open-pose window against y=13 in the closed pose, an 82pt difference on a window 9pt shorter; with the countdown container constants (marginTop 6, height 54, centered content about 70pt) the delivered top inset works out to about 80pt open against about 0 closed, and app/Screen.tsx paddingTop (insets.top + 17) honors it. The name-to-Isha-bottom span is 519.33pt in both poses, so the phantom lands Isha''s bottom at 614.33 against the Settings button top at 568: a 46.33pt overlap (the closed pose clears by 44.67). One mechanism explains both the gap and the overlap. Why: the row''s first criterion names the mechanisms from measurement, and the fix choice depends on the painting layer of each defect.
- 2026-10-20 The Settings button''s anchor is measured real, not phantom: its bottom offset is max(insets.bottom, 20) + 25 in both poses, working out to 59pt, so insets.bottom is 34 (home-indicator class) in both and button top = window height - 101 in both. Why: the fix clamps only the top; the bottom is genuine and stays.
- 2026-10-20 The half-open pose measured: it presents the same 469x669 window with the same phantom (name y=95, Isha bottom 614.33 against button top 568). Why: one fix heals every non-closed pose; Step 2 verifies half-open beside open.
- 2026-10-20 The fix: clamp the honored top inset to zero when it exceeds the largest top inset any iPhone delivers (62, the 16 Pro-class Dynamic Island). A top inset above 62 cannot come from a phone; it names a compat-scaled window, which the system letterboxes precisely so the panel''s bars sit outside the window, so the delivered inset references hazards the window does not contain (the panel''s status bar sits at the panel''s right edge, x about 850-923, outside the window''s x 241-709). Honoring none restores the phone-class rendering the window''s own edges warrant: paddingTop 17, name text in the closed-pose class, Isha clearing the button by about 33.67pt. Why: it is the only consumption site of the phantom (app/Screen.tsx paddingTop; RamadanDecorations reads insets.top but renders only in Ramadan and is out of season; Sheet and ColorPicker read the bottom, which is not phantom), and the signature (an inset no phone delivers) is settings-independent, so no font scale, locale or preference can route a phone into the clamp.
- 2026-10-20 The clamp is a hard zero, not a fit-computed cap. Why, argued twice: a fit cap must predict the column''s height from layout constants that font scale and locale shift, so under accessibility sizes it could bind on a real phone and move phone pixels, which the fence forbids; and the letterboxed window''s own top edge carries no hazard (measured), so zero is the true inset for that geometry, not a compromise.
- 2026-10-20 The clamp lives in shared/insets.ts as one pure function with the 62 constant, consumed by app/Screen.tsx. Why: the repo''s pattern puts pure logic in shared/ with its own test file, and the boundary (62 honored, 63 clamped) gets direct unit tests that kill mutants on both arms.

## Files

Read (evidence for the design, no change):
- app/Screen.tsx (the one phantom consumer: paddingTop = insets.top + SCREEN.paddingTop), app/Navigation.tsx (BackgroundGradients absolute fill; the bottom-anchored Settings button: max(insets.bottom, 20) + 25), components/countdown/Countdown.tsx (container marginTop 6, height 54, centered content), components/prayer/List.tsx, components/day/Day.tsx.
- shared/constants.ts (SCREEN.paddingTop 17, SPACING.xl 20, SIZE.nav.bottomOffset 25, STYLES.countdown.height 54, SIZE.contentMaxWidth 500, COLORS).
- app.json (orientation portrait, requireFullScreen true), plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.js, ios/Athan/Info.plist in the main tree (both arrays Portrait and PortraitUpsideDown, UIRequiresFullScreen true).
- Row 116''s plan doc (doc 20) for the sanctioned geometry and Apple quotes; e2e/README.md Gotchas before measuring; __tests__/app/Screen.test.tsx for the harness the new Screen tests extend.

Changed:
- shared/insets.ts (new): PHONE_MAX_TOP_INSET = 62 and honoredTopInset(top) returning 0 above it, top unchanged at or below it.
- app/Screen.tsx: paddingTop consumes honoredTopInset(insets.top) + SCREEN.paddingTop.
- shared/__tests__/insets.test.ts (new): boundary and arm coverage (0, 20, 47, 62 honored; 63, 80, 100 clamped to 0).
- __tests__/app/Screen.test.tsx: the rendered page pins the clamp at the consumption site (insets.top 80 renders the closed-pose-class padding; 62 renders 79).
- package.json and app.json: the version bump, patch, on every step commit. board/snapshot/board.sql regenerated per step commit. The worktree prebuilds ios only and holds no android tree, so no build.gradle exists in it; the third leg rides app.json into the next prebuild, and the main tree''s gitignored android/app/build.gradle is hand-set to the final version after merge, never committed (row 116''s audited precedent).

## Design

The standing design ruling holds verbatim: one schedule, centred, inside the existing 500pt max content width; no two schedules, no centre settings button, no Duo-branched layout. The Duo''s non-closed poses keep Apple''s sanctioned scaled window with its letterbox. The only behaviour change: a top safe-area inset larger than any phone delivers is not honored, because the compat-scaled window that delivers it is letterboxed by the system exactly so the panel''s hazards stay outside it. The invariant: at or below 62 the function is the identity, so every phone geometry, portrait pose and setting renders exactly today''s bytes; above it the window is scaled and honors none.

Alternatives rejected:
- Vehicles A and B (fill the panel): tabled on the ruling comment; A unfreezes rotation on every physical iPhone where the about-640pt column clips in a about-400pt window, B is Duo-branched code banned by the standing ruling.
- A fit-computed cap (honored = min(top, window height minus column need)): font scale and locale shift the column''s need, so the cap could bind on a real phone and move phone pixels; rejected on the fence.
- Clamping the bottom inset too: the bottom is measured real (34 in both poses, home-indicator class); clamping it would move the Settings button on every phone-class window that shares it.
- Honoring 62 instead of 0 in the scaled window: paddingTop 79 still lands Isha at 596.33 against button top 568, a 28.33pt overlap; the ruling''s overlap criterion fails.

## Steps

1. Step 1: the oversized-inset clamp lands (code, 2 points). Write shared/__tests__/insets.test.ts and the new Screen.test.tsx cases first and watch them fail against the unchanged Screen (red), then land shared/insets.ts and the one-line Screen consumption (green), mutation-check both boundary arms (62 to 63 and the zero return), run yarn validate, bump package.json and app.json one patch, regenerate board/snapshot/board.sql, one commit.
2. Step 2: the ruled outcome verified on the iPhone Duo simulator (device, 3 points). Build from the branch with npx expo run:ios -d SIMULATOR_UDID --configuration Release (SIMULATOR_UDID placeholder in every committed record), then drive and transcribe onto this row: closed pose first as the fence read (window 466x678, name y about 13, Isha bottom about 532.33, button top about 577, equal to the pre-fix reading); open pose (window 469x669, name y in the 13-17 class against 95 pre-fix, Isha bottom about 534.33 against button top 568, clearance about 33.67 against the 46.33pt overlap pre-fix); half-open pose (same class as open); the bars'' pixel columns re-read and recorded as Apple''s letterbox, unchanged; fold liveness (process id unchanged across a closed-open-closed round trip with the overlay reachable); one open-pose screenshot attached to this row from scratch outside the worktree, never committed. Version bump, snapshot, one commit. This step runs alone in a fresh executor session.
3. Step 3: the row''s records closed (docs, 1 point). Read the branch diff and record that it names exactly shared/insets.ts, shared/__tests__/insets.test.ts, app/Screen.tsx, __tests__/app/Screen.test.tsx, package.json, app.json and board/snapshot/board.sql, so no phone-tuned layout value moved; tick the subtasks; regenerate the snapshot. Version bump, snapshot, one commit.

## Risks

- A future iPhone delivers a top inset above 62: it would render the countdown at the plain 17pt offset (cosmetic, the content sits below a taller island). Recorded here, not chased; the constant is named for exactly this read.
- RamadanDecorations reads insets.top directly: it renders only in Ramadan (out of season now) and fades with the overlay; out of this row''s scope. If a future in-Ramadan open-pose reading shows the same phantom there, it is its own row.
- The simulator''s hinge-angle read returns no sample (three attempts this session, matching row 116''s instrument note): every pose claim is confirmed by the lit panel''s viewport and frame dimensions instead.
- The jest Screen harness must mock react-native-safe-area-context''s inset for the clamp test: the executor reads the existing setup and extends it; if the setup mocks at module level, the test overrides per-case with jest.mocked.

## Proof

- Mechanism named: the Decisions section carries the two measured mechanisms with their numbers (pixel columns for the bars; name y 95 against 13, Isha bottom 614.33 against button top 568 for the phantom).
- Gap gone, Isha clears: Step 2''s transcribed open-pose frames (name y in the closed-pose class against 95 pre-fix; Isha bottom below button top with the clearance quoted) on the row.
- Bars remain, recorded: Step 2''s re-read pixel columns beside the ruling comment 2371, which carries the record and the tabled price.
- Phones bit-identical: the branch diff names no phone-tuned value; shared/__tests__/insets.test.ts pins 0, 20, 47 and 62 as the identity; the Screen test pins 62 rendering 79 and 80 rendering the closed-pose class; the closed-pose on-device read equals the pre-fix reading; yarn validate green at every step commit.


## Audit (2026-10-20)

Both reviews on uat..lane/264 came back with the change judged sound and narrowly scoped (comments 2397 plan-conformance, 2398 blind); each suite re-ran green in the worktree by both reviewers. Findings adjudicated:

- Confirmed and fixed in records: the acceptance''s predicted clearance "about 33.67" is replaced by the measured 35.67 (Isha bottom 532.33, the closed-pose span exactly reproduced); acceptance boxes 1 and 5 ticked with the proof they already had.
- Confirmed as a records precision, no code change: the phrase "the one consumption site" (plan Decisions, the Step 1 commit subject) names the one site this row changes. RamadanDecorations reads the same top inset for its seasonal moon and stars and stays untouched by this row''s scope: it is Ramadan-gated, out of season, fades with the overlay, and unverifiable in October; if a future in-Ramadan open-pose reading shows the same displacement there, it is its own row. The clamp applies to it the day one is filed.
- Answered from the plan''s Risks: a future iPhone delivering a top inset above 62 renders the countdown at the plain 17pt offset (cosmetic, content below a taller island). Intended, recorded, not chased; the constant''s name carries the read.
- Rejected, recorded why: un-exporting PHONE_MAX_TOP_INSET (the tests deliberately pin the literals 62 and 63 so a constant edit cannot silently pass; the export documents the boundary''s provenance and the function references it), and trimming the test file''s header (it follows the suite-header convention: module under test named first, the fence restated for the test reader).
- Device readings that closed the row: comment 2390 (open name y 13 against 95 pre-fix, Isha clearing by 35.67 against the 46.33pt overlap, closed pose equal on all three readings, bars pure black and unchanged, fold liveness held, half-open same class as open), screenshot attachment bfe2c279 on the row and never committed.', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, NULL, 20480, 3, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-19 23:17:03.483801+00', '2026-10-20 00:05:55.004578+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (9, 1, NULL, 'T-140', '# T-140: Arabic line height on Android, the gaps the owner sees on glass

## Goal

The owner compared the phones under Arabic and named three gap sites where the 3T sits taller than the iPhone: the Arabic prayer name above the countdown against the countdown, the location against the date on both schedules, and the bottom sheet''s title against its subheading. Wording, lettering and clipping are correct everywhere; only spacing differs. Row 137 established the standing explanation: the same Rubik TTF renders a taller effective line box on Android (nearest the head bbox set, measured 1.578 to 1.633em with a +2 view-bound residual) while the iPhone sits on hhea/typo (1.185em exact). This ticket measures each named site''s rendered line box and inter-element gap on both phones, attributes each gap to the platform line box or to the layout, and brings the two options with exact numbers and blast radius. The ticket ends Owner-led: the owner rules between accepting the platform box (row 87''s law) and pinning an ar lineHeight per surface family (no negative pulling). No code, no pixel change, no install.

## Decisions taken (agent-ruled 2026-10-19)

- **D140.1 Vehicles, read-only.** Drive the installed builds: 3T the store app 2.0.908, XS the store app 2.0.939. Why: the full geometry-bearing diff from both vehicle commits (9dd4eb6a, 6446955f) to uat head over `components/`, `shared/`, `app/` and `assets/fonts/` touches none of the three sites'' geometry - the only component change is Countdown''s UNIT_SIZE_SHARE 0.9 to 0.95 (a unit-letter font size inside the digits row that moves no margin and no line box; the row''s line box is set by the 24pt digits), and the Help, ColorPicker and Settings changes are row 136''s chevron direction pins. The fonts are unchanged. A fresh 2.0.1108 build would read the same numbers at the cost of installing on the owner''s phones.
- **D140.2 Site 1 authority.** The prayer-name node above the countdown is a standing full-width band artefact on both phones (row 137, void on both). The site-1 gap is delivered from ink: the digits'' nodes corroborate, the pixels measure. Ink gaps are the deliverable at every site; node bounds corroborate.
- **D140.3 Site 3 on the XS.** The settings sheet publishes zero accessibility content nodes on the XS (row 137''s void). Pixels are the XS authority for the sheet pair; the 3T''s node bounds corroborate the margin.
- **D140.4 Controls are repeatability.** Row 137''s pin controls proved both instruments (3T Help pair, overlay body on both). This ticket''s control is the re-read: every surface in each step''s control list (step 2: 3T location 67px, date 75px, sheet title 93px, subtitle 58px; step 3: XS location 57px, date 64px, countdown word 61px, digits container 86px) must reproduce within 1px, or the step stops and records the drift.
- **D140.5 Scope of both schedules.** The location-date pair is read on the Standard and the Extras page on both phones; the countdown pair on both pages; the sheet pair on the Settings sheet, the app''s bottom sheet.
- **D140.6 No repo commits.** The record lives on the board. The font-tables script runs from git history (6ff7364d) restored into session scratch, never committed; screenshots never enter git.
- **D140.7 Not-ar arrival (grill finding 9).** If either phone arrives with the app language not ar, the executor records the found state, sets the app''s own language to العربية (an in-app preference, not an OS setting), measures, and restores the found language afterward. The invariant "no OS settings change" stands; the app language is app state, and the record carries the restore.

## Files (read only)

- `components/countdown/Countdown.tsx` - site 1: name 17 Rubik-Regular, marginBottom 3dp, translateY -2; digits 24 Rubik-Medium in the timer row.
- `components/day/Day.tsx` - site 2: location 16 Rubik-Regular with marginBottom 8dp under ar; date 18 Rubik-Regular.
- `components/sheets/parts/Header.tsx` - site 3: title 22 Rubik-Medium with marginBottom 0 under ar; subtitle 14 Rubik-Regular with marginTop 6dp under ar.
- `shared/typography.ts` - the ar record: sizes, faces, the scriptLineHeight pin pattern (round(n x 1.5)).
- `shared/constants.ts` - SPACING (xxs 2, quart 5, py 6, sm 8), TEXT sizes (14, 16, 18, 26).
- `assets/fonts/Rubik-Regular.ttf`, `assets/fonts/Rubik-Medium.ttf` - the metric table, via the row-137 script from history.
- `e2e/device-atlas-oneplus3t.md`, `e2e/device-atlas-iphone-xs.md` - driving routes and instrument notes.
- Row 137''s record in git history: 6ff7364d (expectations), da1b0b7b and f1b7428e (3T), 896fd998 and 4431aaa1 (XS), 9de7215d (adjudication), 4b6dfb72 (verdict and options).

## Design

Row 137''s ladder, extended one rung to the gaps between elements:

1. **Expectation and margin table.** Candidate line boxes per face and size from the shipped TTFs at both scales (3T 2.625, XS 3.0), each set''s ascent and descent (baseline position inside each candidate box), and the code''s margin per site in dp, 3T px and XS pt, with the expected node gap per site: site 1 name marginBottom 3dp (the translateY -2 shifts the name''s own box and ink up inside its margin, expected node gap 3dp, ink gap 3dp minus 2 shifted), site 2 location marginBottom 8dp, site 3 title 0 plus subtitle marginTop 6dp. The margins are platform-independent (dp), so any cross-platform node-gap delta above the margin is already suspect.
2. **Node bounds.** The live accessibility tree per phone: 3T through `maestro hierarchy` (`uiautomator dump` stays refused on Android 9), XS through agent-device snapshot frames. Two independent captures per screen must agree on every named site''s bounds and every static label, byte for byte; the countdown digits node is compared on bounds only, because its accessibility label reticks every second.
3. **Ink bounds, the row-scan contract (per site, per phone).** The executor''s script (PIL, in session scratch) processes one screenshot per screen. Region: the rectangle spanning the site''s two elements, sourced from the published node bounds where they exist; for a void surface the region is anchored on the adjacent published bound extended by the code margin plus the candidate box height (site 1: the digits node extended upward by 3dp margin plus the 17pt name''s candidate box; site 3 on the XS: the sheet chrome bounds from the snapshot tree plus the header''s paddingTop, spanning title and subtitle). Predicate: convert to grayscale; per row, background = the row''s median luminance inside the region; a row is ink-candidate when row median minus row minimum exceeds 80/255 (strong) and is flagged weak between 25/255 and 80/255 (the text shadow is a 0.3-alpha black halo offset 3 down with radius 6, which reads weak). Band rule: consecutive ink-candidate rows merge into one band across gaps under 3 failing rows; bands under 6px tall are flagged as fragments, never dropped. Output: the two-threshold band table per region. The vision worker then names each band (which is the name, the digits, the location, the date, the title, the subtitle, which is shadow or gradient artefact) from the same crop, and the record carries the script bands and the vision names side by side. A band the two rungs disagree on becomes a recorded void, not a guess. Spent digit groups do not render in either installed build (Countdown filters them), so no dimming confound exists.
4. **The gap table.** Per site per phone: node gap (box to box) where published, ink gap (what the owner sees), code margin, and the overhang split - the space above and below the ink inside each adjacent box, measured from ink position inside node bounds where both exist. Predicted overhang cross-check: box top to baseline per metric set is the set''s ascent times size times scale; predicted ink top = baseline minus the measured ink height above baseline, so each platform''s matched set (3T the bbox set with the +2 residual, XS hhea) must predict the measured overhang within 2px.
5. **Attribution.** Two branches, both stated per site. Where the node gap is readable on both phones: the gap difference is the platform line box''s when the node gap equals the code margin on both phones and the Android-minus-iPhone ink-gap delta closes against the overhang deltas within 2px; it is the layout''s when a node gap itself departs from the code margin. Where a node gap is void (site 1 both phones, site 3 on the XS): the margin-plus-box model stands in - the expected ink gap is computed from code margin plus each platform''s predicted overhangs under its matched set, and the measured ink gap attributes to the platform line box when it matches the 3T-set prediction and not the XS-set prediction at the same margin. Each site gets one line naming the branch and the deciding numbers.
6. **The options.** (a) Accept the platform box: each site''s measured cross-platform ink-gap delta, row 87''s law unchanged. (b) Pin an ar lineHeight per surface family, the pin set enumerated per node: name 17 to 20, location 16 to 19, date 18 to 21, title 22 to 26, subtitle 14 to 17 (each round(1.185 x size), the hhea parity the XS already renders), with the digits row (24 Rubik-Medium, Latin digits) carried as a ruled-in-or-out variant: a 24 to 28 pin on the digits side only if the owner wants that side compressed too, both variants priced with numbers. The floor per pin: the measured ink height of that surface''s rendered strings on the 3T, plus 1px; a pin below its floor risks clipping (the ar clamped-blocks precedent in typography.ts) and is not offered - this is the no-negative-pulling rule made numeric. The residual gap each pin leaves (the ink gap after the pin, predicted and stated), and the blast radius: component-scoped (three files, the named surfaces only) against record-scoped (every ar consumer of that face and size, named from step 1''s sweep).

Invariant: no code, no pixel change, no install, no OS settings change; read-only contact; the phones restored as found (language restored per D140.7 if it was touched); screenshots stay in `$HOME/athan-gitree/sessions/140/` and never enter git.

Alternatives rejected:

- Building and installing 2.0.1108 on both phones: rejected under D140.1, identical geometry, a device mutation for no measurement delta.
- Deriving the ink gaps arithmetically from the metric sets without pixels: rejected. The deliverable is what the owner sees on glass; ink is measured, then reconciled against the metric arithmetic as a cross-check.
- Reading only the Standard schedule: rejected, the owner named both schedules (D140.5).

## Steps

1. **Step 1: The expectation, margin and sweep table (weight 1, docs).** Restore the row-137 tables script from 6ff7364d into session scratch, extend SIZES to 14, 16, 17, 18, 22, 24 and extend its columns to print each set''s ascent and descent (hhea, typo, win ascent and descent; head yMax and yMin) so the baseline position inside every candidate box exists; run it against the shipped TTFs. Read the three components'' margins into the expected-gap table (dp, 3T px, XS pt). Sweep every consumer of the measured faces and sizes under ar: `grep -rn "TEXT.sizeSmall\|TEXT.size)\|ty.size(22)\|countdownSize" components/` read down to the rendered face and size per hit, producing the blast-radius table step 5 cites. Proof: the shared sizes (14, 22) reproduce row 137''s EXPECTATIONS.md line-height numbers byte for byte.
2. **Step 2: The 3T readings (weight 3, device, runs alone).** Read-only on the store app 2.0.908. Pre-flight vehicle line: `wm size` (1080x1920), `wm density` (420), `settings get system font_scale` (1.0 expected; a different value stops the step as drift). Verify app language ar on arrival (D140.7 otherwise). `maestro hierarchy` twice per screen on Standard home, Extras home and the settings sheet (day labels, countdown name and digits nodes, sheet title and subtitle); screencap each screen; repeatability controls against row 137 (location 67px, date 75px, sheet title 93px, subtitle 58px, each within 1px); record bounds verbatim with the vehicle line; restore as found.
3. **Step 3: The XS readings (weight 3, device, runs alone).** Read-only on the store app 2.0.939. Pre-flight vehicle line: the home face matches row 137''s layout (location and date present at the top of the list, settings button bottom-centre); the panel is 375 x 812 pt at 3x. Verify app language ar on arrival (D140.7 otherwise). agent-device snapshot frames on Standard and Extras (day labels, countdown digits container), pymobiledevice3 screenshots of both home pages and the settings sheet (the standing AX void makes pixels the sheet authority); repeatability controls (location 57px, date 64px, countdown word 61px, digits container 86px, each within 1px); restore as found.
4. **Step 4: The vision ink reads (weight 1, docs).** Run the row-scan contract''s script per screenshot, then dispatch the vision worker per screenshot with the band table and the crop: name and bound the ink at every site on both phones. Proof: every named ink band sits inside a node bound where both exist (ink under box, row 137''s relation), and every disagreement becomes a recorded void.
5. **Step 5: The attribution and options record (weight 1, docs).** The measured table (each cell the instrument publishes, a named void in the row-120 form where it does not; a site whose ink read voids on one phone stands on the other phone''s read plus the matched-set prediction, and the record names the void), the attribution line per site, the two options with exact numbers and blast radius citing step 1''s sweep, the closing OWNER-LED move with Waiting on set and the two-line recommendation.

## Risks

- The countdown name''s node stays a band artefact: covered by D140.2, ink is the deliverable and row 137 corroborates the rendered word is normal on both phones.
- The XS sheet publishes no nodes again: covered by D140.3, pixels are the standing authority.
- A repeatability control fails: the step stops and records both readings as instrument drift; the ticket reports the confound rather than guessing.
- The mock API names a different prayer or date than row 137''s session: irrelevant to geometry, the record names the word each bound was read under.
- Text shadows and the gradient background blur the row scan: the two-threshold band table plus the vision worker''s naming adjudicates; a disagreement becomes a recorded void, not a guess.
- The day rolls mid-capture (mock evening window): both pages of one phone are captured back to back; a date-string change between captures shows as a repeatability control failure and stops the step.

## Proof

- The plan and record: `list_issue_documents` on T-140.
