## Audit record (2026-10-20)

Plan-conformance (comment 2468): clean on every check - the fence, the comment sweep, the
sensitive sweep, version law, the board window, the step records. One nice: the gitignored
coverage/ artifact carried the worktree home path on disk; deleted in the fix commit so the
clean-tree claim re-verifies literally.

Blind (comment 2467): two shoulds confirmed and fixed at 65094cd5 (2.1.31) - the duplicated
doc clause in shared/widgetTypes.ts the sweep garbled, and the two dated citations inside JSX
comment blocks (PrayerWidget 521, 902) that the plan''s line-anchored regex could never reach;
their companion test-title citation (widgetContract 499) fixed with them. Two nices: the
sourced-names notes dismissed on D54.1 (data keeps its dates - the reviewer itself read them
defensible), the coverage artifact dismissed into the fix. The proof criterion''s regex gap is
this record''s own note: JSX blocks needed the looser pass, and the residual scan now prints
zero outside the ruled-keep classes (docstring examples, mock seeds, the corpus line).

Both reviews clean after adjudication. yarn validate green at 2.1.31 (227 suites, 5871 passed,
2 skipped, coverage 100 percent); the pre-commit discipline hook verified the bump.', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, NULL, 21504, 8, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-20 00:44:32.909031+00', '2026-10-20 07:20:34.449446+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (24, 1, NULL, 'T-276', '# T-276 — Ten bottom-bar arrangements, screenshotted for the owner''s pick

Owner order 2026-10-20. The deliverable is ten screenshots on this row, not merged code: the branch dies after the shots, uat never moves.

## The three elements (unchanged primitives)

The real SettingsButton (43px wide, the real colors, border, shadow, haptics) and the two real 6px schedule dots (Standard/Extra, active/inactive opacity). Every design rearranges only their container. Real mock data behind them (mock build: prayers sit either side of launch).

## The ten designs

1. **One Line (the owner''s fixed spec)** — the button''s bottom edge sits on the line the dots'' bottoms sit on today, clear of the home indicator; the dots flank it left and right, all three vertically centred on that one line: small dot, big button, small dot.
2. **Corner Pair Left** — button bottom-left corner, dots side-by-side bottom-right. Mirrored asymmetry; frees the whole bottom centre. Trade: the dots sit far from the button they orbit.
3. **Corner Pair Right** — the mirror of 2: button bottom-right, dots bottom-left. Same trade, other thumb.
4. **Floating Pill** — one frosted pill centred on the line, holding dot, button, dot inside it. Trade: reads as a single control; the pill adds its own visual weight.
5. **Integrated Capsule** — the button widens into a capsule and the two dots dock inside its left and right ends. Trade: smallest footprint of all; the dots stop reading as pages to some eyes.
6. **Edge Rail Right** — vertical stack pinned to the right edge: button on top, the two dots below it stacked vertically. Trade: the bottom centre is fully clear; dots read top-to-bottom as pages.
7. **Edge Rail Left** — the mirror of 6 on the left edge.
8. **Segmented Track** — a full-width hairline track near the bottom; the button centred on it, one dot at each end. Trade: maximal symmetry; the track anchors the whole screen foot.
9. **Wide Aperture** — button centred on the line, the two dots pushed out near the screen''s side edges at the same height. Trade: the most air; the dots all but vanish until swiped.
10. **Corner Stack** — button bottom-left, the two dots stacked vertically bottom-right. Trade: diagonal balance, the vertical pair doubles as a page ladder; the busiest of the four corner designs.

## Switcher mechanics (debug-only, branch-only)

- Gate: `EXPO_PUBLIC_DESIGN_SWITCHER=1` at build time. Any build without the env renders today''s shipped layout through a byte-identical path: the gate is the default-off branch.
- A debug overlay in the Navigation chrome layer: an invisible full-width tap strip across the top of the screen; each tap advances the design 1 to 10 and wraps. A tiny `Dn/10` badge (top corner, 10px, low opacity) so every screenshot self-identifies from its pixels.
- Revision (device pass): the phone''s tap runner needs an Apple account session this Mac no longer holds, so commit 2.1.35 added a ten-second auto-cycle beside the strip; the badge alone confirms which design each screenshot shows, and the pixel read verified the order.
- No test coverage demanded (throwaway); `yarn validate` stays green so the build works.

## Shot list

- Worktree `$HOME/athan-gitree/worktrees/lane-276`, branch `lane/276` off uat 2.1.33. Author Athan. Never pushed, never merged, deleted at the close.
- No other build may run at once (the Duo lane builds nothing this run; checked before start).
- Mock iOS Release build of the branch with the switcher env, installed on the physical iPhone XS (`IPHONE_UDID`). One throwaway launch after install (What''s New), then per design: tap strip, settle, screenshot, ten times.
- `design-01.png` through `design-10.png` attached to this row in order, one comment per design naming what varies and what it trades. Screenshots never commit to the repository.

## Steps

- Step 1 (code, 2 pts): the switcher and the ten arrangements land on `lane/276`, validate green, version bump per commit.
- Step 2 (device, 3 pts): mock build on the iPhone XS, the ten screenshots attached in order with their comments.

## Audit

Two reviewers on the protocol''s honesty: the shots match the designs, nothing leaked (no screenshots in the repo, no device identifiers outside placeholders), nothing merged. Not code quality of a throwaway.

## Risks

- A dropped tap desynchronises shot order: the badge on every shot makes desync visible from pixels; a mismatched shot is retaken.
- Metro env-blindness: the build runs with a fresh TMPDIR so no stale transform ships without the switcher.
- iOS build time and signing are the long poles; nothing else runs beside them.', NULL, 'compass', '{}', false, false, false, NULL, NULL, 23552, 2, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-20 03:54:08.519408+00', '2026-10-20 05:19:14.381037+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (23, 1, NULL, 'T-271', '# T-271

## Goal

What is wrong today: on the iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1), open pose, iOS letterboxes the portrait-locked app into a system-scaled centred 469x669 window on the 951x669pt inner display, with pure-black bars either side (row 264''s measured record). True when this is done: the plist declares landscape so iOS grants the spanning window, a native gate returns landscape only for a screen of the Duo inner display''s class, every other screen - the Duo''s closed outer display, every physical iPhone, every iPad, every Android device - stays portrait exactly as today, the one centred schedule draws unchanged inside the existing 500pt max content width, and the spanning window''s real insets are measured against the 2.1.24 clamp.

THE RULING (owner, 2026-10-20, on the row): no landscape in general; the one exception is the Duo''s open inner display, to remove the black letterbox bars. The ruling narrowly lifts the no-Duo-branch ban for the orientation gate alone: the gate may key on the Duo''s inner display, the layout may not branch.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-20 The gate is a native AppDelegate override, not expo-screen-orientation''s lockAsync. Apple documents `application(_:supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor:)` as returning "the total set of interface orientations supported by the app", "intersected with the orientations supported by the root view controller", with the plist "as the default interface orientations" when unimplemented (developer.apple.com, UIApplicationDelegate). Expo SDK 58''s `ExpoAppDelegate` declares that method `open` with the comment "It will use the values from Info.plist as the orientation mask unless a subscriber requested a different orientation" (node_modules/expo/ios/Expo/ExpoAppDelegate.swift:243), so a subclass override in the app''s AppDelegate is the sanctioned seam, synchronous before any rotation commits. Argued twice against the alternative: expo-screen-orientation''s own SDK 58 docs say "On iOS 27 and later, requireFullScreen no longer opts your app out of being resized, so it cannot reliably lock the screen orientation" and "lockAsync may have no effect. The system can refuse the request" (docs.expo.dev/versions/v58.0.0/sdk/screen-orientation) - a lock the system may refuse cannot carry the fence that no physical iPhone ever rotates; and the lock is a JS-initiated async call after launch, so between process start and the lock landing the plist maximum governs unguarded. The package is also not installed, and installing a dependency is an ask-first rule the sleeping owner cannot answer.
- 2026-10-20 app.json keeps orientation "portrait"; the new plugin writes the plist maximum. The iPhone array must carry landscape for iOS to grant the spanning window (row 264''s measured mechanism), but flipping the field to "default" would write `android:screenOrientation="unspecified"` (SDK 58 setAndroidOrientation: `orientation !== ''default'' ? orientation : ''unspecified''`) and let every Android device rotate - a direct fence violation. The plugin sets the iPhone array to all four orientations through withInfoPlist, the exact pattern plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.js already uses to pin the ~ipad array. The owner''s recorded consent to the app.json change this row needs covers the plugins-list entry; the orientation field itself does not change.
- 2026-10-20 The gate keys on the screen class: max(bounds.width, bounds.height) == 951 and min == 669 points, either order. The row''s own measured geometry (951x669 inner, 466x678 closed outer); no shipping iPad panel shares the class (mini 744x1133, Air 820x1180, Pro 11 834x1194, Pro 13 1032x1376). Challenged once: a mode reporting scaled screen bounds makes the key miss and the gate fails closed to portrait, never open. Unknown screen or nil window: the portrait pair.
- 2026-10-20 The gate returns [.portrait, .portraitUpsideDown, .landscapeLeft, .landscapeRight] on the Duo inner display and [.portrait, .portraitUpsideDown] everywhere else - byte-equal to the set a portrait-only plist gives every phone today (SDK 58 Orientation.js maps "portrait" to exactly that pair). Failing closed on every unrecognized screen is the fence.
- 2026-10-20 The 2.1.24 clamp (shared/insets.ts honoredTopInset) is re-verified by measurement; no clamp code changes in this row without a fresh owner order. The spanning window delivers real panel insets, not the phantom ~80 the scaled window delivered; if every open-pose reading (landscape, portrait hold, half-open) delivers a top inset at or below 62, the clamp is the identity there and no code changes. If any open-pose reading exceeds 62 (a real spanning inset the clamp would eat), the row parks in Awaiting Owner with the transcribed reading and the one-line question, and the exemption lands only on the owner''s order. Why, argued twice: the ruling lifts the no-Duo-branch ban for the orientation gate alone, and an inset-honoring branch keyed on the panel would be a second Duo keying in shared code that the ruling does not cover - agent-ruled landing would exceed it; and a panel-class key cannot tell the spanning window from a scaled one on the same panel (row 264''s record has the half-open pose presenting the scaled window with the phantom), so any future exemption must be scoped to the spanning geometry, which only the owner can order with the reading in hand.
- 2026-10-20 No layout change lands. app/Screen.tsx already centres the schedule (maxWidth 500, width 100%, alignSelf center); the wide window only widens the margins. Nothing in JS learns the pose.

## Files

Read (evidence for the design, no change):
- ios/Athan/AppDelegate.swift and ios/Athan/SceneDelegate.swift in the main tree (the prebuild products; AppDelegate subclasses ExpoAppDelegate, and the iOS 27 SDK mandates the scene life cycle through ExpoAppSceneDelegate).
- node_modules/expo/ios/Expo/ExpoAppDelegate.swift (the open supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor at line 243), node_modules/@expo/config-plugins/build/ios/Orientation.js ("portrait" => [Portrait, PortraitUpsideDown]), build/android/Orientation.js ("default" => "unspecified"), build/ios/RequiresFullScreen.js (the iOS 27 resizing note).
- plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.js and plugins/__tests__/replacePreviousNotification.test.ts (the repo plugin pattern: pure edit functions, exported sources, literal-pinning tests).
- app/Screen.tsx (the clamp consumption site), shared/insets.ts, shared/constants.ts (SIZE.contentMaxWidth 500), app/Navigation.tsx, app.config.ts (plugin resolution).
- Plan doc T-264 for the measured geometry, the phantom record and the Vehicle A/B table; e2e/README.md Gotchas before any measurement.

Changed:
- plugins/duoLandscapeGate.js (new): withInfoPlist setting UISupportedInterfaceOrientations to the four-orientation maximum; withAppDelegate inserting the gate into class AppDelegate through an exported pure editAppDelegate that is idempotent and throws when its marker is absent; the Swift gate exported as a source constant.
- plugins/__tests__/duoLandscapeGate.test.ts (new): written red against the absent plugin.
- app.json: "./plugins/duoLandscapeGate" added to plugins directly after ./plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.
- shared/insets.ts, app/Screen.tsx and their tests: only on the owner''s order after a Step 2 reading above 62 parks the row (Decision 5).
- package.json and app.json version bump (patch) on every commit; board/snapshot/board.sql regenerated per commit. The worktree prebuilds ios only; the main tree''s gitignored android/app/build.gradle is hand-set to the final version after merge, never committed (row 116''s audited precedent).

## Design

The plist carries the maximum; the delegate narrows per screen. iOS reads the iPhone orientation array to grant the spanning window on the Duo''s inner display (row 264: "iOS grants a spanning window only to an app that declares landscape"); at runtime UIKit asks the app delegate for the orientations of the specified window, and the returned mask is intersected with the root view controller''s before any rotation commits. The gate returns the four only when the window''s screen is the Duo inner display class, the portrait pair otherwise, so:

- Duo open, inner display: spanning window; landscape and portrait holds both render edge to edge; the one centred schedule keeps its 500pt column; the black bars are gone because the window is the panel.
- Duo closed, outer display: portrait pair, byte-equal to today.
- Physical iPhone, iPad, every other class: portrait pair, byte-equal to today; the plist maximum is invisible because the delegate mask is the authority at every rotation decision.
- Android: untouched (orientation stays "portrait"; screenOrientation stays "portrait").

The invariant: on every screen except the Duo inner display class the app''s supported set equals today''s portrait-only plist exactly; on that class the window widens and nothing else changes.

Alternatives rejected:
- Vehicle A (landscape in the plist, no gate): unfreezes rotation on every physical iPhone, exactly what the ruling forbids.
- expo-screen-orientation lockAsync: refused by the system on iOS 27+ per its own docs, asynchronous after launch, and a new dependency behind an ask-first rule.
- A root-view-controller gate in the SceneDelegate: wrapping the root VC the factory builds disturbs the tree Expo owns; the delegate override is the seam Expo itself documents, and Apple''s scene-migration page moves only the four life-cycle methods to the scene delegate, leaving the orientation method with the app delegate.

