## Goal

What is wrong today: on the iPhone Duo''s open inner display the app never renders on a tall canvas. A portrait-locked launch gets the system-scaled centred 469x669 compat window on the 951x669pt panel with pure-black bars either side (row 264''s measured record), and Option A''s landscape spanning was cancelled by the owner''s final ruling before any of it merged. The owner''s pick is Option B: the open Duo held rotated 90 degrees, the inner display a tall 669x951 canvas, the two panels stacked as top half and bottom half across a horizontal fold seam, no left and right, the app''s existing portrait layout rendering top-down, background edge to edge, no letterbox.

True when this is done: the tall hold renders the app portrait on the full tall canvas edge to edge with no letterbox, the Duo''s closed pose stays portrait, no other device rotates, phones render unchanged, and Option A''s branch never merges. If Apple''s OS refuses the portrait-tall presentation, the row sits in Blocked carrying the documented refusal and the transcribed readings, with no workaround attempted.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-20 D1 The mechanism is Apple''s pose-driven presentation, named from the docs; no API requests the tall canvas. Apple''s Tech Talks 111461 (Prepare your app for iPhone Duo): "Interface orientation on the inner display behaves differently. The inner display doesn''t honor your supported interface orientations." WWDC26 session 278 (Modernize your UIKit app): "In iOS 27, an app''s supported interface orientation is a preference provided to the system. It will be ignored when your app is running in a resizable environment," and UIRequiresFullScreen "no longer opts your app fully out of resizing. Instead, it enables discrete resizing that honors your supported interface orientations." The prepare page (developer.apple.com/iphone-duo/prepare/): fixed orientations and hardcoded screen sizes are the breaking patterns, and an iOS 27 SDK-current app "resizes dynamically on iPhone Duo." The tall canvas arrives as a device pose, produced by rotating the device, never by code. Challenged: UIWindowScene requestGeometryUpdate(.iOS(interfaceOrientations: .portrait)) asks the system directly - rejected because row 271 measured that ask "received yet refused" on this panel, and the Tech Talks line names the inner display as ignoring orientation preferences; an ask the OS refuses is not a path, and forcing it is the hack the owner banned. Committed: the plist carries the maximum, the pose carries the canvas.
- 2026-10-20 D2 Measure before writing code. Row 264 measured the compat letterbox in the landscape hold only; the tall hold was never measured (row 271''s closing revision records the portrait hold as "not producible by rotation on this panel" while its own commit reads the same read as "beyond the simulator''s instruments" - the row does not distinguish a tool gap from an OS refusal, and its asks were portrait-direction). Whether today''s portrait-only build already spans tall in the tall hold is unknown, and it decides whether any code lands: if the OS presents the tall canvas to today''s build, Option B is the OS''s own presentation of the shipped app and nothing merges. Challenged: always landing the orientation opt-in would produce evidence one session sooner - rejected because it risks landing a landscape-hold change the owner cancelled and spends a code step that may implement nothing. Committed: Step 1 measures today''s build first, and Steps 2 and 3 carry exact no-op records when it grants the tall canvas.
- 2026-10-20 D3 The code shape, if code lands: a prebuild plugin in the pattern row 271 established, named plugins/duoTallCanvas.js. withInfoPlist sets UISupportedInterfaceOrientations to the four-orientation maximum - row 264''s measured mechanism: the spanning window is granted at launch only to an app that declares the panel''s pose orientation. withAppDelegate inserts an override of application(_:supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor:) returning the portrait pair for every window, with no Duo keying anywhere in the gate. The owner''s final ruling ("this row becomes Option B''s implementation") is the recorded order covering the app.json plugins-list entry the implementation needs; the orientation field itself does not change, keeping "portrait" so Android''s screenOrientation never moves (row 271''s Decision 2 reading applied to this row''s ruling; SDK 58 maps "default" to android:screenOrientation="unspecified"). Challenged: keying all-four on the 951x669 panel class exactly as row 271 did - rejected because that keying exists to present landscape on the Duo, the rendering the owner cancelled; the keying-free portrait pair keeps every screen''s effective set byte-equal to today''s portrait-only plist, so whatever the inner display presents is the OS''s per-pose choice, not a branch this repo chose. Committed: keying-free. Two records carried from row 271''s audit for this gate: the gate''s presence sentinel keys on its own selector inside class AppDelegate, because a template-provided supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor would silently disarm the override while the plist still grants four; and the override bypasses Expo''s subscriber manager, harmless while no orientation-requesting module is installed.
- 2026-10-20 D4 The landscape hold is transcribed, never engineered, and it never decides Done on this plan''s own authority. If the plist maximum lands, the landscape hold may present spanning landscape as the OS''s per-pose presentation - a rendering the owner cancelled as the Duo''s default posture. The owner''s ruling pins the tall hold, the closed pose, other devices and phones; it does not order the OS to letterbox the landscape hold, and manufacturing that letterbox would be the hack the ruling bans. Committed: the tall hold must meet the acceptance criteria; the landscape hold''s reading is recorded on the row either way; and a gated build whose landscape hold presents spanning landscape parks the row in Awaiting Owner, Waiting on carrying the reading and the screenshot, before any Done - the default-posture rendering is the owner''s call, not the plan''s. If the tall canvas proves reachable only through the Duo-keyed all-four mask row 271 carried - the cancelled gate''s bytes - the row moves to Blocked with that evidence instead of landing it.
- 2026-10-20 D5 The rotation instrument is agent-device''s orientation command (orientation <portrait|portrait-upside-down|landscape-left|landscape-right>, documented "Set device orientation on iOS and Android") beside fold for the poses; row 271''s records do not establish whether its portrait-direction asks were refused by the OS or reached no instrument, so this row drives rotation fresh in all four directions and transcribes the canvas class each value reports. Device Hub''s rotate buttons (named in Tech Talks 111461) are the fallback when the command refuses or no-ops at the tool level. The Duo simulator is this row''s: row 271 closed Done with the cancellation recorded, its worktree and branch deleted.
