## Goal

The What''s New modal lists every feature the app ships except itself: the archive holds no item for the What''s New screen, so the changelog never tells a reader the screen exists. When this is done the archive carries an item for the What''s New screen, stamped with the release that first showed the modal, its title and body rendered through t() in all six locales.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-25 - The debut stamp is 1.13.0. Git history names it: df08bfb1 (2026-08-30, "1.13.0 - feat(whats-new): post-update What''s New modal") is the first commit carrying shared/whatsNew.ts and shouldShowWhatsNew, and no earlier commit carries the feature. This follows the T-297 restamp ruling: stamp the release the feature truly shipped in, named from git history.
- 2026-10-25 - The item stamps below the archive''s newest feature stamp (language, 2.0.37), so shouldShowWhatsNew''s newest-stamp bar does not move: an upgrader who has seen the language item never gets a pop from this change. The acceptance is the archive listing the item, not a pop (T-306''s content-driven gate reads only the newest stamp).
- 2026-10-25 - Keys whatsNew.title.whatsNew and whatsNew.body.whatsNew; en copy "What''s New" / "Added a note about what each update changes" (10 and 43 characters, inside the 32/96 limits).
- 2026-10-25 - The commit subject is typed feat(whatsNew): the changelog is a user-facing surface and gains its first self-describing row, so the bump takes the minor 2.5.0 above uat''s 2.4.43, patch reset per the bump rule.
- 2026-10-25 - android/app/build.gradle is gitignored prebuild output (T-297 audit finding), so the bump touches package.json and app.json only.
- 2026-10-25 - The merge commit takes 2.5.1 and moves the WHATS_NEW banner stamp with it: the oracle test pins WHATS_NEW.version to app.json''s expo.version on every commit.

## Files

- shared/whatsNew.ts - the new archive item; the banner stamp rides the version bump.
- shared/i18n/en.ts, ar.ts, ms.ts, so.ts, hi.ts, th.ts - the two new keys after the language pair, translated in each.
- shared/i18n/catalog-lock.json - rewritten by yarn i18n:lock (a deliberate en catalog change whose translations landed in the same commit).
- shared/__tests__/whatsNew.test.ts - the debut pin tests, written red first.
- package.json, app.json - the version bump.

## Design

One archive item, { titleKey: ''whatsNew.title.whatsNew'', bodyKey: ''whatsNew.body.whatsNew'', version: ''1.13.0'' }, placed after the language item at the head of the items array. No display code changes: the resolver, the limits contract, the translation lock and the grouping pick the item up by construction, and 9 items sit inside the 20-entry window.

Alternatives rejected:
- Stamping at the current release: it would move the gate''s newest-stamp bar and pop the modal for every upgrader, which the acceptance rules out.
- Any visual or heading change: visuals are settled, and the acceptance is an archive item.

## Steps

1. Step 1: the What''s New debut item and its six-locale copy, pinned red-first (2 points, code). One commit: the red pin tests observed failing, then the catalog keys in six locales, the archive item, the lock rewrite, the version bump and the banner stamp; yarn validate green; mutants killed by name (debut stamp 1.13.0 to anything else, a locale copy reverting to en).

## Risks

- A locale''s copy runs past 32 or 96 characters: the per-locale limits test fails by name.
- The ar copy mixes Latin or echoes en: catalogGates fails by key.
- The lock is not rewritten: the catalogLock test fails on the changed en hash.
- A bump without the banner stamp move: the oracle test fails.

## Proof

- git log --follow --reverse --format=''%h %ad %s'' --date=short -- shared/whatsNew.ts shows df08bfb1 2026-08-30 "1.13.0 - feat(whats-new)" as the feature''s first commit: the stamp''s git-history name.
- yarn jest shared/__tests__/whatsNew.test.ts: the debut pin test passes, and was observed red before the item landed.
- yarn validate green.
