INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (44, 1, NULL, 'T-388 plan: the foreground widget push must not fail the sync', '# T-388 plan: the foreground widget push must not fail the sync

## Goal

Today `initializeAppState`''s non-deferred (foreground) branch awaits `PrayerWidgets.refreshPrayerWidgets()` bare (stores/sync.ts:259). A push that rejects walks into `sync()`''s catch, which rethrows: a foreground return or background-task body whose widget push throws fails the whole sync while good cached days sit in storage. When this is done, a sync whose widget push rejects completes on every path, warns one line through the Pino logger exactly as the deferred path does, and no push rejection can fail a sync that holds usable days.

Scope, stated plainly (grill finding 1 answered): this fix removes the one unguarded widget-push await in the sync, so no sync path can fail on a push rejection. The ticket''s intake record pins the mimic''s blanked home screen to this bare await (owner order 2026-10-27). The home screen''s error page reads `syncLoadable`, which never runs the foreground path, and the foreground caller catches its own rejection (`device/listeners.ts:69`), so this lane claims the guard''s contract above, not a proven device causal chain; the loadable cannot flip to `hasError` from a foreground rejection on current code. The relaunch persistence on that build implicates its launch path, which the existing deferred catch guards on current code. If the symptom returns on a device, the log lines this fix pins (`SYNC: Failed`, `WIDGET: ...`, `LISTENERS: ...`) name the rejecting path, and that read belongs to the lead''s audit, not this plan.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-27: The foreground catch warns with its own message, `WIDGET: Foreground refresh failed`, at warn level with the `{ error }` payload, mirroring the deferred line''s shape. A distinct string names the path that fired; a shared string would leave an incident log unable to say which path lost the push.
- 2026-10-27: The catch swallows the rejection (no rethrow). The sync completes; the widget stays stale until the next push (sync, notification refresh, settings change), the same tolerance the deferred path applies. This honors the code''s own rule that only a launch with nothing usable stored may reach the error screen.
- 2026-10-27: The new tests live in `stores/__tests__/syncLoadable.test.ts`, the suite that mocks the logger and already pins both push paths. `stores/__tests__/sync.test.ts` keeps its awaiting test untouched.
- 2026-10-27: `stores/widget.ts` is read, not changed. Its internal catches make a rejection rare, but the bare await''s contract is what fails the app; the deferred path already established the outer guard as the pattern.
- 2026-10-27: The version bump set is `package.json`, `app.json` and `shared/whatsNew.ts` (`WHATS_NEW.version`). `android/app/build.gradle` is prebuild-generated and untracked in git; the repo''s bump commits touch exactly those three files.
- 2026-10-27: One green commit carries tests, fix and bump. Red-first is proven in the worktree (tests fail with the fix absent) before the commit; a red test commit would land a failing suite on uat.
- 2026-10-27, grill finding 2: the regression guard is the pair of direct assertions (the foreground sync resolves; the warn fires). The acceptance''s `syncLoadable` `hasData` read stays in the test because the ticket''s acceptance names it (verbatim, the owner''s words), recorded here as documentation of the home screen''s read, not the guard: the loadable never runs the foreground path, so that clause cannot fail. Deleting it is the owner''s call, not this lane''s.
- 2026-10-27, grill finding 1: no device-log capture step is added. This lane runs no device (the lead owns device work); the honest scope is the guard''s contract, stated in Goal.

## Files

- `stores/sync.ts` — the fix: `initializeAppState`''s non-deferred branch catches the push rejection and warns.
- `stores/__tests__/syncLoadable.test.ts` — the new foreground-rejection test.
- `package.json`, `app.json`, `shared/whatsNew.ts` — version 2.11.20 riding the commit.
- `stores/widget.ts` — read only (the push''s own guards).
- `device/listeners.ts` — read only (the foreground caller, whose own catch keeps a rejected sync off the render tree; its `LISTENERS: Foreground sync failed` error line stops firing for a push rejection once the guard lands).

## Design

The non-deferred branch of `initializeAppState` becomes:

```ts
} else {
  try {
    await PrayerWidgets.refreshPrayerWidgets();
  } catch (error) {
    logger.warn(''WIDGET: Foreground refresh failed'', { error });
  }
}
```

Landed as try/catch rather than `.catch` on the call''s return: the awaited guard states its intent directly and does not depend on the callee''s return being a promise (the sync suite''s widget mock returns undefined, which `.catch` on the call would throw on before the guard could act).

The invariant: the widget push is best-effort on every path. The deferred path already catches; the background task and the foreground return share this non-deferred branch, and both keep the await (iOS holds the process alive until the push settles). The branch''s comment gains one clause naming the foreground catch''s reason: a push failure must not fail a sync that holds usable days.

Alternatives rejected:

- Guarding inside `refreshPrayerWidgets` (widget.ts) instead: it already guards each platform push internally; adding an outer swallow there would also silence the deferred path''s own catch point and move the failure policy out of the sync, whose rule ("only nothing-stored reaches the error screen") is what the bug breaks.
- Making `sync()` catch around `initializeAppState`: that would also swallow sequence and countdown failures, which are the app state itself and belong on the error screen.
- A shared `pushWidgetsTolerantly()` helper for both branches: the branches differ by await and defer mechanics, and a helper called from inside a `requestAnimationFrame`/`setTimeout` hop obscures the timing the comment documents.

## Steps

1. **Step 1: the foreground-rejection test** in `stores/__tests__/syncLoadable.test.ts` (2 points, code). One test drives `sync({ deferWidgetRefresh: false })` with `mockRefreshPrayerWidgets` rejecting. The regression guard, both direct: the promise resolves, and `logger().warn` is called with `WIDGET: Foreground refresh failed` and the error. The acceptance''s home-screen read follows: `triggerSyncLoadable` then `store.get(syncLoadable)` lands `hasData` (documentation, per the decision above). Red before the fix: the resolves assertion fails on current code.
2. **Step 2: the guarded foreground push** in `stores/sync.ts` (2 points, code). The non-deferred branch catches and warns per the design; the comment names the reason.
3. **Step 3: proof and merge** (2 points, code). Coverage on the touched lines, mutation pass on the new guard (catch removed, warn demoted, message changed, payload dropped: each fails a test), `yarn validate` green, version 2.11.20 riding the commit, merge the lane branch into local uat unpushed. Mutation pass run on the guard: catch removed (the red run), warn demoted to info, message changed, payload dropped, swallow changed to rethrow — each fails the new test.

Sum: 6 points.

## Risks

- Swallowing hides a push that keeps failing: the warn line is the observable, and the widget self-corrects on the next push.
- The background task body runs this same branch; after the fix a failed push no longer fails the task. The await remains, so iOS still holds the process until the push settles.
- The fix cannot be claimed to cure the mimic''s observed blanking end to end: the loadable never runs the foreground path (Goal, scope). The log lines it pins are what a recurrence will be read against.
- The loadable read in the test cannot go red on its own, so the direct resolves assertion carries the regression guard; the mutation pass proves the test set fails with the fix absent.

## Proof

- Criterion 1''s guard: `yarn jest stores/__tests__/syncLoadable.test.ts` green, and the new test fails with the fix reverted (the sync rejects). The acceptance''s `hasData` read is asserted in the same test.
- Criterion 2: the same test asserts `logger().warn` called with `WIDGET: Foreground refresh failed`, `{ error: failure }`.
- Criterion 3: the deferred describe''s two tests (`settles the sync before the push`, `logs a failed push`) pass unmodified.
- `yarn validate` green; the coverage report covers the new catch.', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, NULL, 44032, 3, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-27 10:43:45.623622+00', '2026-10-27 10:59:47.649231+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (40, 1, NULL, 'T-346 - the prayer-ago badge keeps its space around the time in every language, and Hindi stays on one line on Android', '# T-346 - The prayer-ago badge keeps its space around the time in every language, and Hindi stays on one line on Android

## Goal

Today two visual defects sit in the time-ago badge below the prayer list. Thai on both platforms renders the sentence''s trailing word touching the interpolated time''s last letter with no space: `shared/i18n/th.ts`''s `time.ago` is `''{name} {duration}ที่แล้ว''`, so the byte after `{duration}` is a Thai consonant, while the other five locales space the token or end the string at it. Hindi on Android only wraps the badge into two lines with the second cropped to a sliver, because the badge Text in `components/prayer/Ago.tsx` carries no one-line constraint and Android''s text engine breaks the sentence at a space. When this is done, every shipped locale''s `time.ago` template holds a space (or the string''s edge) on both sides of the `{duration}` token - gated for all six locales and any future one - and the badge never wraps: one line on every platform, the pill growing into the room it has.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-25 - The spacing rule is enforced as a catalog gate over every locale''s `time.ago` template, not in `compose`. Why: the interpolated time is the `{duration}` token of one key; injecting spaces at composition would change every interpolated string in the app (channel names, steppers, reminders) across six locales, while the gate holds the owner''s general rule - any text adjacent to the time is separated by a space - for every catalog the key set admits, present and future.
- 2026-10-25 - The gate accepts the string''s edge as a valid neighbor. Why: Arabic ends the sentence at the time (`''{name} قبل {duration}''`) and the owner''s reference read lists Arabic as spacing correctly; the defect is text touching the time, and a string edge touches nothing. Thai''s defect is a consonant byte, which the gate refuses.
- 2026-10-25 - The one-line mechanism is `numberOfLines={1}` on the badge''s Animated.Text. Why: it is the platform''s own never-wrap contract; the pill already grows with its content (`alignSelf: ''center''` under the page''s full width), so the constraint buys horizontal growth in the room the owner names. `adjustsFontSizeToFit` is iOS-only and would change glyph sizes; a maxHeight clip would hide bytes; both rejected. T-345''s rejection of a numberOfLines guard was for a title the owner reserved; here the owner''s own acceptance line orders the one-line constraint.
- 2026-10-25 - The badge''s inner component is exported for the suite. Why: `PrayerAgoBadge` already exists as the unit boundary that takes the composed string; the suite renders it under all six locales without the store, the clock or the locale switcher. Precedent: `calculatePrayerAgo` is exported "so tests can call the real thing" (hooks/usePrayerAgo.ts:19).
- 2026-10-25 - The device read is Step 3 and waits for the build slot. Why: the owner''s reference reads are the S23 (Thai, Hindi) and the XS (Thai); a mock build (both platforms, `build-mock.zsh`) driven on those phones is the final proof of the rendered badge; Lane 1 (T-327) holds the device slot and the step waits rather than contending. The unit layers prove the string and the constraint; the phone proves the pixels.
- 2026-10-25 - The bump path is package.json + app.json + `shared/whatsNew.ts`''s `WHATS_NEW.version`, not android/app/build.gradle. Why: `/android` is gitignored prebuild output (recorded in T-345''s plan, verified with `git check-ignore`), so no commit can carry it; app.json plus prebuild is the real path. A bump that adds no whats-new item silent-ships by construction.
- 2026-10-25 (execution) - `components/prayer/__tests__/Ago.test.tsx` already existed at base with seven badge tests; the plan had named the suite new. Why recorded: the suite grew additively (header note, imports, one describe) after the executor restored its clobber, and the pre-existing tests stand green untouched.
- 2026-10-25 (execution) - uat moved to 2.5.1 (lane3/T-348''s merge) after this branch''s base at 2.4.44; the branch stamps 2.4.45 and 2.4.46 carry no twin on any lane, and the merge renumbers above whatever uat then carries, the standing ruling.
- 2026-10-25 (agent-ruled, step 3) - The Android read moves from the S23 to the OnePlus 3T. Why: the S23 is not attached today and the 3T is the only other Android phone connected; both panels are ~360dp wide, so the same Hindi badge wraps or holds identically, and the 3T is the e2e floor device. The owner''s reference screenshots stay the S23''s.

## Files

- `shared/i18n/th.ts` - the one-byte fix: `''time.ago''` gains a space after `{duration}`.
- `shared/__tests__/catalogGates.test.ts` - the new spacing gate over every locale''s `time.ago` template.
- `components/prayer/Ago.tsx` - `numberOfLines={1}` on the badge''s Animated.Text; `PrayerAgoBadge` exported for the suite.
- `components/prayer/__tests__/Ago.test.tsx` - new suite: six locales'' composed ago strings render under the one-line constraint.
- `package.json`, `app.json`, `shared/whatsNew.ts` - the patch version bump each commit carries.
- `scripts/version-collisions-allowlist.json` - an entry if another lane''s stamp twins mine (record the interleaving, renumber above whatever uat carries).
- Read, proven untouched: `hooks/usePrayerAgo.ts` (composes the string), `shared/i18n/index.ts` (`compose`, `tIn`), `shared/time.ts` (`formatTimeAgo` wraps the duration in U+2066/U+2069 isolates), `__tests__/app/Screen.test.tsx` (the badge''s existing coverage), `shared/__tests__/i18nBridge.test.ts` (token parity - tokens unchanged).

## Design

The invariant, in two halves.

Spacing: the only place the time value enters user-facing copy is the `{duration}` token of the `time.ago` catalog key (the badge''s `time.now` half interpolates the prayer name only; `usePrayerAgo.ts:38`). The rendered duration arrives from `formatTimeAgo` wrapped in Unicode isolates (\u2066...\u2069, zero-width), so whatever byte neighbors `{duration}` in the template neighbors the time''s first and last visible glyph on screen. The gate therefore reads the template: for every catalog (en, ar, ms, so, hi, th), the byte before `{duration}` is a space or the string''s start, and the byte after is a space or the string''s end. Today th fails on the trailing side and no other locale fails; the fix is the single space in th''s template. The gate joins the R2 gates in `catalogGates.test.ts`, which every locale must already pass to land, so a seventh locale that touches the time inherits the rule - general, never language-specific.

One line: the badge is an `Animated.Text` styled as a pill (`alignSelf: ''center''`, `paddingHorizontal`, `borderRadius: pill`, `overflow: ''hidden''`) under the page''s full width. It carries no line constraint, so Android''s text engine is free to break the Hindi sentence at a space and crop the second line against the pill''s radius; iOS does not break it. `numberOfLines={1}` removes the engine''s freedom: the Text lays out its content on one line and takes that width, the pill grows to wrap it, and on iOS - where every locale already renders one line - the constraint changes no pixel. The suite renders `PrayerAgoBadge` once per locale with that locale''s own composed ago string (via `tIn`) and asserts the rendered Text carries `numberOfLines={1}` and shows exactly the composed string, so the constraint is proven against every locale''s real bytes, Thai''s isolates included.

Alternatives rejected: injecting spaces in `compose` (blast radius across every interpolated key, above); a per-locale width table (the ticket bans language-specific rules); `adjustsFontSizeToFit` (iOS-only, changes glyph size); `maxHeight` clipping (hides bytes rather than refusing the wrap).

## Steps

1. **Step 1: the time-ago spacing gate and the Thai byte (2 points)** - the gate in `catalogGates.test.ts` over all six locales'' `time.ago` (red on th''s trailing edge first), then the one-byte fix in `th.ts`, then the mutation pass: revert the Thai byte and watch the gate fail; strip a leading space from a compliant locale in memory and watch it fail on the leading edge; put the token at both string edges and watch it pass. Version bump 2.4.45, renumbered above whatever uat carries at commit time.
2. **Step 2: the one-line badge constraint and its suite (2 points)** - export `PrayerAgoBadge`, add `numberOfLines={1}`, and write `Ago.test.tsx`: for each of the six locales, compose the ago string with `tIn` and assert the badge renders exactly that string under `numberOfLines={1}` (red first: the prop is absent today); mutation: remove the prop and watch the suite fail. `Screen.test.tsx` and `usePrayerAgo.test.ts` stay green untouched. `yarn validate` green. Version bump, same renumbering rule.
3. **Step 3: the device reads on the 3T and the XS (3 points)** - wait for the build slot (Lane 1/T-327 holds it; confirm no build runs: `pgrep -fl ''assembleRelease|xcodebuild''`), build mock both platforms via `$HOME/athan-gitree/bin/build-mock.zsh`, read the device atlases (`e2e/device-atlas-oneplus3t.md`, `e2e/device-atlas-iphone-xs.md`), switch the app''s language to Hindi and Thai, and transcribe: Hindi''s badge on one line on the 3T with no cropped second line, Thai''s badge with a space before and after the time on both the 3T and the XS (S23 amended to 3T, agent-ruled, above). Readings land on the ticket; no screenshot is committed; no tree change, no bump.

## Risks

- A user font scale far above the fleet''s could make a one-line badge wider than the screen; the constraint then clips rather than wraps. The owner''s rule orders one line; the fleet''s scales render every locale''s badge well inside the width.
- The gate reads template bytes, not shaped glyphs; a locale whose script shapes a space away (none shipped) would need the gate read again. All six shipped scripts keep the space byte.
- If `getByText` on an `Animated.Text` does not surface `numberOfLines` through props in this harness, the suite reads the prop off the matched element the way `Screen.test.tsx` reads style props; the fallback is asserting through the element''s own props where the prop is set.

## Proof

- Spacing per locale: `npx jest catalogGates` - the new gate names each locale and th passes only with the space; red before green recorded in the step.
- One-line constraint per locale: `npx jest Ago` - six locales, the Text carries `numberOfLines={1}` and renders the composed string.
- Whole suite: `yarn validate` (tsc --noEmit && biome check . --error-on-warnings && jest --silent --coverage) green.
- Rendered badge: Step 3''s transcribed reads on the 3T (Hindi one line, Thai spaced; the S23''s stand, agent-ruled) and the XS (Thai spaced), posted on the ticket.
