INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (41, 1, NULL, 'T-368', '# T-368

## Goal

Google and Apple hold the 2.4.39 production builds for review and the public release waits on the owner''s go. Today nothing proves on glass the binaries users will download. When this ticket closes, the exact store artifacts are downloaded from EAS, their identities recorded, what each phone install displaces is measured and written here, and the owner holds a go/no-go verdict carrying every fact it needs.

## Decisions taken

1. 2026-10-25 - The release awaiting review is the FINISHED STORE pair at 2.4.39, git commit 340bd90e on main: Android AAB build 11 (EAS id 8bcf3319-b5b2-4a8e-b335-9ca08353a97c) and iOS IPA build 74 (EAS id 0d1e373d-ecd8-4acf-b688-b74985429deb). The 2.4.38 pair (04018984 Android, d161d042 iOS) errored in the Install dependencies phase and produced no artifact, so no store saw them. Evidence: the EAS build list plus git dc269768 naming both store builds shipped from main at 2.4.39.
2. 2026-10-25 - The Android artifact is an AAB, so the installable APK comes from bundletool build-apks run on the downloaded AAB. bundletool repacks the modules with the derivation Play itself performs and compiles nothing; the package id and the EAS signing ride untouched. No bundletool exists on this Mac (PATH, Android Studio plugins and the gitree searched), so the pinned official jar is fetched into the scratch directory and its sha256 recorded on the ticket. Rejected: aapt2 directly on the AAB (the bundle format refuses it); compiling 2.4.39 locally (the owner''s order forbids it and it would prove nothing users get).
3. 2026-10-25 - The 3T install is tabled, never performed this run. The 3T''s <pkg> is the owner''s local prod line: versionName 2.4.40, versionCode 1000000, Android Debug certificate fac61745dc0903786fb9ede62a962b399f7348f0bb6f899b8332667591033b9c, installed for User 0 alone. The store APK carries the EAS upload key at versionCode 11. The signature mismatch blocks adb install -r, pm uninstall -k keeps the package setting that blocks a differently-signed reinstall, and adb restore verifies signatures and refuses. Every path to the store line on the 3T destroys the owner''s app data, so the exact commands and their cost land on the ticket and the owner rules. Step 3 transcribes the measured refusal.
4. 2026-10-25 - The iPhone XS attempt is gated on the profile read. The store IPA embeds an App Store distribution profile that names no device, and devicectl refuses such installs. embedded.mobileprovision is decoded first: when it names no device, the devicectl attempt runs once to transcribe the refusal (the same profile gates the device-side check, so no displacement is possible); when it names the XS, the attempt is skipped and the install joins the owner decisions beside the 3T. iOS glass of the exact payload rides Apple''s re-sign through TestFlight, which only the owner performs.
5. 2026-10-25 - The athan-smoke-28 emulator smoke of the store-derived APK is included as functional evidence of the exact Android binary: the AVD is the repo''s sanctioned smoke lane for install cycles, no owner line sits on it, and the artifact''s id and signing ride intact. It is never cited as phone or performance evidence. The emulator is killed at the end of the step.
6. 2026-10-25 - The AGENTS.md fleettest badging guard runs: aapt2 dump badging on the derived APK, the package id recorded on the ticket. The literal stop is superseded for these two artifacts by the owner''s 2026-10-25 order naming the store artifacts on the phones, and the displacement analysis still lands before any install act.
7. 2026-10-25 - No worktree. Nothing touches the repository; downloads and tooling live under $HOME/athan-gitree/store-verify-T368/. No version bump rides because no commit exists.

8. 2026-10-25 - Correction, recorded after Step 3 measured it: the 3T''s line moved mid-run. A lane-2 mock install (10:53) took <pkg> from 2.4.40 to 2.4.46 at versionCode 1000000, the same repo debug certificate; the owner''s prod data sits untouched in its own storage namespace. Both install attempts were refused with INSTALL_FAILED_VERSION_DOWNGRADE (the version gate fires before the signature gate), which sharpens the owner''s data-keeping path: adb install -r -d of the repo-keystore derivation keeps every byte and costs only a reversible line downgrade to 2.4.39.

## Files

No repository file changes. Reads:
- eas.json, app.json - profiles, submit targets, versions.
- e2e/README.md - the measurement gotchas (the dev-env confound; on a real-data run the alarm dump is the evidence).
- e2e/device-atlas-oneplus3t.md, e2e/device-atlas-iphone-xs.md - phone lines, signing, driving notes.
- $HOME/athan-gitree/bin/build-prod.zsh - the local line''s package id, certificate, versionCode.

Scratch, outside the repository: $HOME/athan-gitree/store-verify-T368/ (the AAB, the IPA, the bundletool jar, the derived APKs, the transcripts).

## Design

Verification runs artifact-first: pin what the stores hold, download exactly that, derive the installable form without compiling, then measure each phone''s install feasibility without displacing anything. The Android functional read comes from the disposable emulator running the store-derived APK. The phones'' glass installs are the owner''s rulings, each carrying the exact command and its cost.

Invariant: EAS is read-only (list, info, download). Nothing is built, pushed or submitted anywhere. No owner line on any phone is displaced. No screenshot and no device-reported place reaches any record. Transcripts only.

Alternatives rejected: compiling 2.4.39 locally (the order forbids it); installing the store APK over the 3T line (destroys the owner''s data, ruled an owner decision); skipping the emulator smoke (would leave the verdict without one functional read of the exact binary).

## Steps

1. Step 1: The reviewed pair pinned (2 points) - build_info for both 2.4.39 STORE builds read through the expo MCP and recorded on the ticket: ids, versions, build numbers, git commit, artifact URLs, expiration dates.
2. Step 2: The artifacts in hand (2 points) - AAB and IPA downloaded from the EAS URLs into $HOME/athan-gitree/store-verify-T368/, sha256 recorded for both; the pinned bundletool jar fetched; the universal APK derived and read (aapt2 dump badging, apksigner certificate digest); the IPA unzipped and read (Info.plist bundle id, CFBundleShortVersionString, CFBundleVersion; embedded.mobileprovision decoded, its device list and expiry transcribed).
3. Step 3: The phones measured, nothing displaced (3 points) - 3T: the installed line recorded (versionName, versionCode, signer, users), the derived APK''s certificate compared against the installed line''s, one adb install -r attempt transcribed (INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE expected), the installed line re-read unchanged after. iPhone XS: the installed line recorded (2.4.46 build 1, the local team-signed dev line), the gated devicectl attempt transcribed.
4. Step 4: The exact binary smoked on the emulator (3 points) - athan-smoke-28 booted headless, the store-derived APK installed, the first sync against the real API observed, today''s times read, the countdown observed running, the yarn check:device alarm dump transcribed, the qibla surface opened; the emulator killed after. Recorded as emulator evidence, never phone evidence.
5. Step 5: The verdict on the ticket (1 point) - each phone''s install decision with the exact commands and their cost, the TestFlight path for iOS glass, the go/no-go framing carrying the emulator evidence; the ticket moves to Awaiting Owner.

Points: 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 1 = 11.

## Risks

- An artifact URL expires or corrupts in transit: re-fetch and verify with unzip -t plus a second download''s sha256.
- The bundletool fetch fails or the jar refuses the local JDK: Step 2 stops and the lane reports what is needed; no substitute compile happens.
- The derived APK fails to install on the emulator: the installer''s error line is transcribed and the smoke reported stopped. The AVD runs the repo''s pinned image, so a real mismatch is a finding, not a tooling gap.
- devicectl reports a refusal different from the profile prediction: transcribed either way. The install succeeds only when Apple''s own profile says it can, which the decode already rules out.
- The owner rules an install mid-run: the ruled install runs on the named phone and the smoke moves from the emulator to that phone; both paths are already written.

## Proof

- The artifacts match the reviewed release: Step 1''s build_info lines beside Step 2''s aapt2 badging line (package <pkg>, versionName 2.4.39, versionCode 11), the IPA plist lines (2.4.39, build 74), and both sha256 values, on the ticket.
- What each install displaces is on the ticket: Step 3''s transcripts, the 3T''s before and after dumpsys lines with the INSTALL_FAILED line between them, the XS''s profile decode and the devicectl refusal.
- The smoke pass is transcribed: Step 4''s transcript with the sync read, the day''s times, the countdown observed, the check:device dump, the qibla read, each a command''s output or a transcribed screen read, never a committed screenshot.
- The verdict sits on the ticket: Step 5''s closing comment.
- The repository stands untouched: git status clean at the close, yarn validate run only when a change somehow landed.', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, NULL, 40960, 3, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-25 14:17:10.421899+00', '2026-10-25 17:41:51.513898+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (45, 1, NULL, 'T-396 plan: the failed check reads as failed and stays retryable', '# T-396 - the Check for updates row must tell a failed check from an up-to-date verdict, and stay retryable

## What is broken, on the code as it stands

The owner pressed Check for updates with the network unreachable and the row answered as if the store had spoken. The data layer is honest: a failed store read returns `upToDate:false` (the fetch throws, `getStoreVersion` catches and answers `false`, the AbortController caps the wait at 10 seconds). The row''s defect is what it does with that answer, and it has two halves:

1. **No failure state exists.** `handleCheckUpdatesPress` maps a check that reached no verdict onto the row''s resting look (`setUpdateRowState(result.upToDate ? ''upToDate'' : ''idle'')`), so a failed check is indistinguishable from never having pressed. (The ticket''s finding names the green tick; on the code as merged at 2.11.25 the failed read settles the silent rest. Either way the row claims nothing and shows nothing - the honest failure state is missing, and a cellular fallback under a Wi-Fi-off phone produces a genuine verdict, which is the likeliest glass mechanism for the tick read.)
2. **The budget is spent by the press, not by the verdict.** `checkSpentRef` is set on the press itself, so a failed check leaves the row dead until the sheet closes - exactly the anti-retry the owner rules out.

## The design decisions, argued

### D1. The result shape grows a third field - the genuine gap in device/updates.ts

`UpdateCheckResult = { modal, upToDate }` cannot carry the owner''s ruling, because two different outcomes collapse onto the same pair:

- iOS, the store was never read: `{ modal: false, upToDate: false }` - a **failure**, the budget must return.
- Android, Play answered and started its flexible update: `{ modal: false, upToDate: false }` - an **update-found verdict**, the budget stays spent and Play''s overlay owns the message.

The ticket demands opposite budget semantics for these two, so the pair must be extended. `failed: boolean` joins the result, true exactly where the check ran and still reached no verdict:

- iOS `storeVersion === false` (fetch threw or timed out): `failed: true`
- iOS the lookup answered but no comparison ran (empty results, unreadable installed version): `failed: true` - no verdict was reached, the row may retry
- iOS both verdicts (up to date, update-found/modal): `failed: false`
- Android Play answered either way: `failed: false` (a verdict - current, or update-found with the flexible flow started)
- Android Play rejected the check (`answered: false`): `failed: true` - the ticket''s Android parity case
- the outer catch: `failed: true`
- the 24-hour throttle refusal: `failed: false` - nothing ran and nothing is claimed; the manual press never sees this path

Nothing else in device/updates.ts moves: the timeout stays 10 seconds, the launch check in app/index.tsx reads `result.modal` alone, and every stamp keeps exactly the write it makes today - the three write-nothing failure paths (the iOS store read returning false, Play left unanswered, the outer catch; the first and third still costing an automatic check an hour, not the day), the verdict paths keeping their full-day stamp, and the empty-results failure keeping the success stamp its path already writes before returning - that stamp is frozen as landed, not moved, so the automatic 24-hour check''s behaviour is untouched.

### D2. The row''s fourth look, mapped from the settles

`UpdateRowState` gains `''failed''` beside idle, checking, upToDate:

- **verdict up-to-date**: the green circle, the white tick, the ring gone - unchanged from T-381.
- **verdict update-found**: the existing sheet-close + modal path on iOS, and on Android the row rests while Play''s overlay owns the flow - unchanged landed behaviour; both are verdicts, the budget stays spent.
- **failure**: the circle red with a white X (the owner''s spec, mirroring the green tick''s construction - `#dc2626` over `#ef4444`, the same red-600/red-500 pair the green uses from green-600/green-500), the X drawn by the existing `close.svg` at the tick''s own 9x9 and ink, the two-arrow refresh ring **stays on screen** so the row reads retryable, and the label reads the failure copy. The left download glyph is replaced by the X, exactly as the tick replaces it - the badge is the state, the ring is the affordance.

The label chain: checking copy while checking, up-to-date copy on the green settle, failure copy on the red settle, the resting copy otherwise.

### D3. The budget: spent by the press, returned by a failure

The spend stays on the press (it is what blocks a second concurrent check while one is in flight - dropping that would let two checks race), and a failure hands it back: `if (result.failed) { checkSpentRef.current = false; setUpdateRowState(''failed''); return; }`. The session counter is untouched - a failure''s answer whose sheet closed mid-flight still lands nowhere, and the dismiss handler still re-arms and rests the row. Net effect, in the owner''s words: the once-per-opening budget is consumed only by a verdict, never by a failure; the retry needs no sheet close.

### D4. The spin rests at its authored angle

The ring now survives a settle, and a stop mid-turn would rest it at an arbitrary angle. The spin effect snaps the shared value to 0 whenever it is not checking, so the failure state''s ring first-frames in its settled pose (the house rule: only value changes animate).

### D5. The Arabic face

The row already mirrors whole: logical edges swap the badge and the ring, the ring''s wrapper carries `scaleX: -1` and a mirrored clockwise turn is the anti-clockwise read. The X is a symmetric glyph under the same construction as the tick, so the mirror needs no new rule; the failure label carries its own Arabic string, and the suite pins the Arabic failure read beside the English one.

## The failure copy (drafted, the owner confirms on glass)

Key `settings.updateCheckFailed`, beside the row''s existing three strings, matching their lengths and register:

| Locale | String |
| --- | --- |
| en | Failed to check. Try again |
| ar | فشل التحقق. حاول مجددًا |
| so | Hubintu way fashilantay. Isku day mar kale |
| hi | जाँच असफल रही. फिर से कोशिश करें |
| ms | Semakan gagal. Cuba lagi |
| th | ตรวจสอบไม่สำเร็จ ลองอีกครั้ง |

The English carries both halves of the owner''s direction ("Failed to check" / "try again") in one short sentence; the translations follow the same shape. The catalog lock re-hashes (`yarn i18n:lock`) once the six are in.

## Alternatives rejected

- **Spend the budget only on a verdict (move the spend out of the press).** Same net behaviour, but the in-flight guard then needs a second condition on the checking state, and the press-time spend is what the existing suite pins. The refund is the smaller, more honest diff.
- **Show the failure as a toast or the update modal''s cousin.** The owner specced the row itself: the badge, the label, the ring. Nothing else opens.
- **Retry automatically on a failure.** The owner said tappable, not self-firing; an auto-retry loop over a dead network spins forever.
- **Reuse an error colour role from COLORS.** The green settle deliberately carries one row''s own constants with a comment saying so; the red mirrors that construction rather than minting a palette role for one row.

## Risks

- **The exact-shape suite pins.** `device/__tests__/updates.test.ts` asserts `toEqual` on every return, so every row gains `failed` - that is the point (both sides of the verdict mapping pinned), but it touches ~15 assertions.
- **The Settings suite''s default mock** returns the bare `{ modal: false, upToDate: false }` pair - a shape the data layer can no longer return once `failed` exists. The default becomes an up-to-date verdict (so the two spent-budget tests keep testing a verdict''s spend, not a failure''s refund); the failure tests set their own mock.
- **Coverage is at 100 percent** and must stay there: the new branch (the refund, the failed render arm, the snap) is small and fully pinnable in the two suites.

## The steps

1. **Step 1 (docs, 1 point): this plan** - the design, the copy, the grill, the gate to Ready.
2. **Step 2 (code, 2 points): the row''s fourth state** - red first: the Settings row test rewritten to expect the failure state over a failed check (it settles the silent rest today, so it goes red); then `failed` in `UpdateCheckResult` with every path mapped, the row''s failure state with the red badge, white X, kept ring and failure label, the press-refund with the verdict-only spend, the spin snap, the six catalogs with the lock re-hashed, and both suites pinning every changed line (the three settles, the refund, the retry, the Arabic failure read, the Android answered:false path); mutation pass on the refund and the failed mapping; `yarn validate` green at full coverage; the version rides the 2.11.26 patch (package.json, app.json, shared/whatsNew.ts; origin fetched first).
3. **Step 3 (device, 3 points): the glass read on the iPhone XS** - airplane-mode press reads the red X and the retry label, a second press re-checks, connectivity restored reads the real verdict; the owner confirms the six-locale copy. The lead''s phase, after audit.
