reader''s number scan finds no dependency there.

`yarn validate` passes on the branch: tsc clean, Biome clean, Jest 5785 tests in 223 suites,
coverage 100 percent. Only `package.json` and `app.json` are version files in git. `android/`
is ignored, and its `versionName` was moved with the pair.
', NULL, 'file-text', '{}', false, false, false, NULL, 5120, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-19 01:45:39.728814+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document_revision (id, document_id, version, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, "position", owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, created_at) VALUES (9, 7, 1, NULL, 'Research: moonsighting, parts 1 and 2', '# Moonsighting research, sob 1: sources, deliverable, shared rules

Status: finished 2026-09-14. Findings: `ai/features/moonsighting/RESEARCH-FINDINGS.md`. Sob 2
extends this brief. The rules below bind it too.

v2.0 takes the app beyond London. Today''s API is a slightly modified version of Khalid Shaukat''s
base timings, the derivation behind London''s unified timetable. The research calculates nothing
itself: it understands the method, and picks or writes the right client, before any v2.0 design
starts.

## Sources, in order

1. Every page of <https://www.moonsighting.com>, including `moon.html` and `about-us.html`.
   Enumerate the page list first (crawl, sitemap, link-walk), then read every page. Download every
   PDF the site carries, read each one in full and keep them in the session scratchpad. If the
   site cannot be read, stop and do nothing else.
2. <http://www.hizbululama.org.uk/articles/english/Unified.pdf>, London''s unified timetable.
   Work out the exact modifications of the base timings: which prayers, what offsets or rules,
   and why. That delta is what the app ships.
3. The implementations, read at the source, not the README:
   - PrayerTimeAPI <https://github.com/PrayerTimeResearch/PrayerTimeAPI>. Endpoint
     `https://www.moonsighting.com/time_json.php`, with
     `https://moonsighting.ahmedbukhamsin.sa/time_json.php` the fallback its code calls. It
     takes `year`, `tz`, `lat`, `lon`, `method` (0 Hanafi general, 1 Hanafi Shafag Abyad,
     2 Shafi Shafag Ahmar, 3 Shia Jafari), `both` (the README says 0 or 1, the code sends
     `false` or `true` and shows the second Asr only for methods 0 to 2) and `time` (0 for
     24-hour, 1 for 12-hour). Establish whether the endpoint still answers, which parameters
     reproduce the site''s published tables, how it behaves at high latitude, and whether it
     can serve a worldwide v2.0 directly (finding 43: London only, no city parameter).
   - mawaqit <https://github.com/mawaqit/prayer-times-moonsighting>. Establish what it and the
     endpoint compute, where they agree or diverge, and whether either matches the London
     modification.
4. NPM packages implementing this method. Other moonsighting committees exist: a "moonsighting"
   package may follow a different body entirely. Verify each candidate traces back to Khalid
   Shaukat''s committee and moonsighting.com. Record provenance: author, algorithm source, last
   publish, licence, and whether its numbers reproduce the site''s. The owner''s lead, unverified:
   the `adhan` package''s `CalculationMethod.MoonsightingCommittee()`. Treat it as a candidate,
   not an answer. Establish which paper or page it derives from, whether its Fajr and Isha
   reproduce the site''s tables for the same coordinates and dates, how it handles high latitude,
   and what is approximation rather than method. Diff its output against the site''s tables for
   several cities and seasons, including one above 60N, and against the London unified times.
   Read implementations with the `opensrc` CLI: the source is the arbiter, not the docs.

## Deliverable

- How Fajr and Isha are derived, the high-latitude behaviour, what "moonsighting" contributes
  beyond a solar-depression angle, and where the thesis is documented.
- Who maintains the method, who uses it, and how the published timetables are generated.
- The exact delta between London''s unified times and the unmodified method.
- Which package is safe to depend on, and if none is, what implementing it would take.
- What this implies for a worldwide v2.0: findings 43 (London-only endpoint, no city parameter),
  44 (Magrib has no midnight-crossing rule), 46 (London-pinned test oracles), 47 (high latitude).

## Rules for both sessions

- Own session, clean context. Research, not coding.
- Read 100% of every source: no slices, no `head`, no summary in place of reading. Diff-read
  near-duplicates.
- Never copy, average or invent a prayer time. Report deltas in minutes, not verdicts.
- The API is the source of truth. The app edits nothing it returns.
- Research only: no production code, no builds, no jest, no EAS. Never touch `uat` or
  `releases.json`.
- Never print, store or commit the London Prayer Times API key. The local `.env` holds only a
  placeholder. Ask the owner for the key if the API must be called. Commit through the
  key-prefix guard.


---


# Moonsighting research, sob 2: read, decide, then finish

Status: deferred by the owner, queue row 25. He has not read the findings or answered section
5''s questions. Whether the research is conclusive, and whether v2.0 can use it, stays open.

This extends `ai/prompts/moonsighting-research.md`: its sources, deliverable and rules apply here.

## Where everything is

- Findings: `ai/features/moonsighting/RESEARCH-FINDINGS.md`. Read it first, in full.
- Notes, data and page source: `ai/features/moonsighting/notes/`, `data/` and `artifact/`.
- The visual findings page is private and its link lives off-repo, in the owner''s keep. Rebuild
  it with `artifact/build_artifact.py`. Chart data: `artifact/chart-data.json`, which the
  script reads from the `~/athan-gitree/research/` copy.
- Scratch (crawl, PDFs, raw responses, scripts) is in `~/athan-gitree/research/`, outside git. The repo
  holds everything needed to continue.

## Do, in order

1. Help the owner read it. Before any new research, give him a short, plain answer drawn from the
   findings: what is settled and what is not, and whether each part of the v2.0 plan can rest
   on it. The London API stays London''s default. Moonsighting.com is a second option in London
   and the only source elsewhere. Mark anything unverified, then wait for the owner.
2. The remainders in findings sections 3.1 to 3.4, once the owner wants the research resumed:
   reproduce London''s Asr independently, measure the day-early clock change in Palestine, look
   into the McMurdo polar-edge runs and the Sky Prayers apps, and run the Rust, C# and Dart
   ports only if a decision depends on their numbers. Read `ai/features/global-prayer-times/RESUME-FROM.md`
   (queue row 42) first. Some remainders may already be answered there.
3. Review. Run one independent reviewer over the whole findings file. It attacks every claim,
   count and fixture, re-derives the London counts from `data/london/` and the section 2.13
   dates from `notes/method-versions/`. Fix what it finds. Republish the findings page at the
   owner''s private link.
4. Section 5''s eleven questions stay open until the owner is ready. Bring them only when asked. Do not press.

## Sob 1''s short answers (step 1 starts from these)

1. How is London''s unified timetable calculated, and who set it? The sun times come from HMNAO
   (sections 2.14 and 2.15): sunrise minus 3, noon plus 5, sunset plus 3, Asr at one and at two
   shadow lengths. Fajr and Isha are fixed minute intervals by calendar date, from the 1987-88
   Blackburn observations (Hizbul Ulama, Miftahi''s Tables 5 and 6) with London''s own edits.
   Adopted 1 August 2011 by the mosques named in Unified.pdf. Unanswered: who made the edits, why.
2. Is it safe for the worldwide option? No. The intervals were observed at one latitude
   (53.45N), in one hemisphere, and are meant for the UK.
3. What should the worldwide option use? Sob 1 recommends calculating the method on the
   device (adhan''s `MoonsightingCommittee`) rather than calling the endpoint. It needs owner
   rulings on polar days, and on whether a calculation counts as a source under the
   never-synthesise rule (section 5, questions 1 and 7).
4. Where do mosque clocks, mawaqit and other apps get their times, and do they add offsets? Not
   researched beyond section 2.16, where mawaqit''s PHP copy implements the seasonal function
   only. Candidate research for this session.

## Rules beyond sob 1''s

- Agents: one at a time, two at most. Sob 1''s four parallel agents hit the session limit
  twice and the weekly limit once. Agents save notes after every step.
- Git: the rules on a research worktree and branch, on `--no-verify` (the pre-commit hook runs
  the full jest suite) and on pushing nothing predate the merge of `research/moonsighting`. The
  planning session asks the owner which still apply.

## Prompt to start the session

```
Read ai/prompts/README.md for the standing rules, then ai/prompts/moonsighting-research-2.md.
Read ai/features/moonsighting/RESEARCH-FINDINGS.md in full. Start at step 1: tell me plainly
what the research settles, what it does not, and whether the v2.0 plan can rest on it, then
wait for me. Research only. Follow the rules in moonsighting-research.md. Never invent a prayer
time. Never store or commit the API key. Merge and push nothing without my go-ahead.
```
', NULL, 'file-text', '{}', false, false, false, NULL, 6144, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-19 01:45:39.780332+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document_revision (id, document_id, version, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, "position", owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, created_at) VALUES (10, 8, 1, NULL, 'Programme: SDK 58', '# SDK 58 programme briefs

The queue table in `ai/plans/README.md` is the authority for order and status. This file keeps
only the briefs open rows still cite: section 16 (queue row 18) and D4 (queue rows 38 and 39).
DONE records live in git history.

## 16. SDK 58 stable re-pin + full release-notes review (DONE 2026-10-17, row 111; the ride-`next` policy carries on)

**Trigger.** The owner ruled 2026-10-09: ride the `next` tag and repin to the newest version every
time, release candidates included. SDK 58 hit `latest` 2026-10-16 and row 111 ran the re-pin.
The next attempt takes the newest `expo` at ITS start: `58.0.14` was already on npm at this
ticket''s close (published after this attempt''s step 1 pinned `58.0.13`, the newest at that
moment), so the next re-pin starts from 58.0.14 or newer.

**Landed set (2.0.908, merge `9dd4eb6a`).** `expo ~58.0.13` with its `bundledNativeModules`
delta (eight direct pins moved, three transitive followed: `babel-preset-expo`,
`expo-application` 58.1.1 to 58.1.2, `expo-modules-core`), `react-native` 0.88.0. All three
patches regenerated whole at `expo-background-task+58.0.11`, `expo-location+58.0.17` and
`expo-widgets+58.0.20`, no hunk hand-merged; `didRegisterTaskForTesting` and `mLastAzimuth`
left the patches; the vacuous `local-maven-repo` check died; the guard suite
`shared/__tests__/backgroundTaskPatch.test.ts` pins the offline option on all three sides.
`expo-widgets@58.0.19` was broken upstream for Android (its source imports
`io.github.jakex7.peek.*` while its dependency packages `io.github.expo.peek.*`, 16 unresolved
references at `:expo-widgets:compileReleaseKotlin`); 58.0.20 fixes it, and that defect is why
the first attempt became a NEEDS REPLAN. Proven: `yarn validate` and `yarn test:tz` green at
100 percent, the 3T release build in 517 s with every channel, permission and armed-alert check
passing and the alarm-clock sub-block intact (41 future alerts), the armed production fire at
01:53:00.002 on the second (flags 0x3), and the XS loop green per the row 111 record (cold
start, compass at ~105 delegate calls a second, both language directions, BGTask re-submission,
two mock-build notification fires at their armed seconds with the TIME SENSITIVE level, the
live day-roll cascade). The store-release path is open again.

**Babel 8 rider, re-measured 2026-10-16, still blocked upstream.** `@babel/core` 8.0.7 is
latest; `babel-preset-expo@58.0.17` (the preset `expo@58.0.13` pairs with) depends on 36
`@babel/*` 7.x plugins (among them `@babel/preset-typescript ^7.23.0`,
`@babel/plugin-transform-modules-commonjs ^7.24.8`, `@babel/plugin-transform-react-jsx
^7.28.6`) and `@react-native/babel-preset@0.88.0` depends on `@babel/core ^7.25.2`. A Babel 7
plugin under Babel 8 throws `BABEL_VERSION_UNSUPPORTED`. The four `@babel/*` devDependencies
stay at `^7.29.x` and the rider is re-attempted at every re-pin.

**Scope.**

1. `npx expo install --check` against `latest`: name and bump everything it reports, never `--fix`.
2. Read the full release notes and act on anything new that touches us. Already ruled at past
   re-pins, do not re-litigate: no SwiftPM (CocoaPods stays), no Live Activities. The "no
   threadIdentifier" ruling is superseded by row 120''s order.
3. Full regression: `yarn validate`, `yarn test:tz`, e2e, 3T release build, the dumpsys and
   punctuality gates.
4. Reopen the store-release path: nothing ships to a store or to production before the re-pin
   row is DONE.
5. Bookkeeping: the stack line in `README.md`, the patch rows in `ai/UPSTREAM-PRS.md`, this
   section. (The `ai/AGENTS.md` stack tables were deleted by the owner''s compaction order at
   `b2096c4a` and stay gone.)

**Pin-set and build facts.**

- `react-dom` equals `react`, and `@react-native/metro-config` and `@react-native/jest-preset`
  equal `react-native`, at every re-pin.
- Three local Android modules hardcode `compileSdk 37`, and only a release build notices:
  `checkReleaseAarMetadata` fails the release build only.
- `expo-in-app-updates` is pinned exactly `0.12.0` and is not an SDK package (its peer range on
  expo is `*`); it compiles Kotlin against `expo-modules-core` symbols. Do not patch it.
- On the 3T the alarm-clock proof is a sub-block, never `flags=0x9`. An alarm-clock arm reads
  `window=0 flags=0x3` with an `Alarm clock:` sub-block. `flags=0x9` is the windowed form shown
  by the 8T and the Find X8.
- The SDK-57 known-good backup branch''s last tips are reflog-only (`ef33661b`, `c9614692`).
  Restoring it is the owner''s call.

**Facts the re-pin needs (A1 to A11).** Each cites proof that still stands in the working tree.

- **A1. Upgrade policy, two parts (owner ruling).** Take the absolute latest and fix breaks in
  the code, never pinning back or editing a test to pass. A package broken upstream at its
  latest rolls back alone, one version at a time, to an exact pin with no local workaround,
  until a newer one ships.
- **A2. The Babel 8 blocker** is the rider above. It is this row''s second job.
- **A3. Deliberate divergences from Expo''s pin set** (at the 2026-10-16 stable re-pin,
  `expo@58.0.13`): `react-native` 0.88.0 stable now matches the SDK''s own pin, so no
  divergence survives there. `@expo/ui` and `expo-widgets` are back to exact pins (`58.0.20`
  each, `package.json`): the preview-era 58.0.5 pin that once protected the Android widget
  stub (row 26) broke iOS at dyld launch because the 58.0.5 ExpoUI binary references a
  `_uiRuntime` symbol the newer ExpoModulesCore no longer exports. Upstream closed the trap:
  the stub now exports `memo` as a passthrough, and the loaded-bundle suite is green. Durable
  lesson: `@expo/ui`''s iOS framework tracks ExpoModulesCore''s Swift ABI, so the pair moves
  with the SDK, never against it.
- **A4. `@expo/log-box` is invisible to Expo checks**: it is not in `bundledNativeModules.json`,
  so no Expo check reports it. It sits at `~58.0.12` (`package.json`) on the `^58.0.12` peer
  range that `expo-router` and `@expo/metro-runtime` declare. Move it by that peer range.
- **A5. The commit order that kept the tree green.** One commit: the SDK packages, the rebuilt
  patches and the type fixes the bump forces (the pre-commit hook runs `tsc`). Then React,
  `react-dom` and React Native with the two `@react-native/*` dev tools. Then Reanimated with
  worklets.
- **A6. Rebuilding a patch.** `git apply --check` the old patch on the fresh package. Apply it,
  or make a rejected hunk''s change by hand. Run `npx patch-package <name>` and delete the old
  file. Then delete the package folder and run `yarn install --force` to prove a clean install.
  All three patches are rebuilt at this row (`ai/plans/54-patches-and-copy/FINDINGS.md`,
  section 1), and each rebuild is proven on a phone, never by a green build alone. Removing the
  `publication` block from `expo-module.config.json` is what forces autolinking to compile the
  patched Kotlin from source: with the block present, autolinking resolves the prebuilt AAR and
  Gradle never compiles the patched code.
- **A7. patch-package log semantics.** After a version bump the install succeeds whatever the
  patches did. A `warning` means the old patch still applied. An `error` means that package
  runs UNPATCHED (`shared/__tests__/widgetOpenAppPatch.test.ts:24`).
- **A8. An ESM-only dependency is transformed to CommonJS in BOTH Jest projects, and
  `--experimental-vm-modules` is rejected.** Jest 30''s `require(esm)` is gated on
  `canResolveSync()`, which the components project fails for its custom resolver
  (`jest.config.js:62`). The CommonJS transform both projects share is `jest.config.js:20`.
- **A9. Reanimated 4.7.0 ships the new layout-animation engine as the default.** The legacy
  proxy flag `USE_LEGACY_LAYOUT_ANIMATIONS_PROXY` is `false` in the installed tree
  (`node_modules/react-native-reanimated/src/featureFlags/staticFlags.json`) and no override
  is set anywhere in the repo. Draw sites that ride it: `components/modals/Modal.tsx:81` and
  `:90`, `components/modals/Help.tsx:75`, `components/sheets/screens/Qibla.tsx:61`.
- **A10. After the install, diff the pin set and read every peer warning.** Job 23 measured
  26 packages and installed 25. `react-native-screens` surfaced only as an `expo-router` peer
  warning (`package.json:73`). One warning is expected: `jest-expo > jest-watch-typeahead@2.2.1`
  wants Jest 29.
- **A11. In `widgets/PrayerWidget.tsx` a fixed `frame()` comes before a flexible one, and no test
  can see the order.** `@expo/ui` types `frame()` as two overloads that cannot be mixed, so
  each mixed call became two chained modifiers. Reversed, the greedy frame takes the space
  first and the row changes size (`:657-658`, `:729`).

- expo-notifications 58.1.0 grew `enableRemoteNotifications: false` for local-only
  apps: a candidate for the next app.json pass, still unclaimed at 1.29.321.

## D4. Localization for v2.0 (NOT PLANNED, carried by queue rows 38 and 39)

Owner goal: the app goes global in v2.0 with a language switcher. Research lives in
`ai/plans/39-localisation/`. Rows 38 and 39 carry the owner''s decisions: a typed English
catalog and `t()`, no i18n library, no second language first. RTL is the real work.
', NULL, 'file-text', '{}', false, false, false, NULL, 7168, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-19 01:45:39.797851+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document_revision (id, document_id, version, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, "position", owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, created_at) VALUES (11, 1, 2, NULL, 'The queue', '# The queue

A ticket is a queued unit of work. A step is a plan step. The board and the repository use the
same two words.

The board is the source of truth. There is no queue file in the repository.

## The columns

| Column | What it means |
| --- | --- |
| Backlog | Queued and not yet planned. |
| Deferred | Deliberately parked behind a later version. |
| Planning | A planner is answering the ticket''s frontier and writing the plan. |
| Ready | The plan passed its gate and the ticket waits for execution. |
| In progress | A lane is executing it. The column warns at three, the standing parallel limit. |
| Blocked | It cannot move until something outside the run changes. |
| Audit | Execution finished and the change is under review. |
| Owner-led | The deliverable is a recommendation the owner must rule on. |
| Upstream | Waiting on a party outside the repository. |
| Done | Merged to `uat` with its records. |

## Needs

A ticket that cannot start before another is finished carries a `blocked_by` relation to it. The
relation is a warning, never a lock: the lane rule is that a ticket is runnable when every issue
that blocks it is Done.
', NULL, 'book', '{}', false, false, false, NULL, 1024, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-19 01:47:05.998109+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document_revision (id, document_id, version, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, "position", owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, created_at) VALUES (38, 15, 2, NULL, 'T-126', '# T-126

## Goal

An OWNER-LED row once had to read as awaiting the owner in a read-only mirror this repository no
longer carries (deleted with the queue migration, commits 4c413365 and ae0d2081). The live board is
the itsaplan stack in board/, which renders the columns itself. This ticket verifies that every
part of the old reading guarantee has a live carrier, records the mapping, and closes without
rebuilding removed code or patching the product.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-19: Close by verification, extend nothing. The generator, template, reader and tests this
  ticket names were deleted with the queue migration; the guarantee now lives on the itsaplan board
  and in the workflow skill''s pinned discipline. Why: every part of the old contract has a carrier.
- 2026-10-19: Do not patch the itsaplan product. Its column UI is upstream''s; a product change is a
  VERSION bump in board/env, an owner-visible deployment change. Why: the owner approved the product
  as it renders (T-144) and approved the migration that removed the mirror.
- 2026-10-19: Record the mapping on the board, not in the repository. Why: a ticket''s documentation
  lives on the board and dies with the ticket; the discipline''s pinned home is
  .agents/skills/board-workflow/SKILL.md, which already carries items 6 and 9 and the picking rule.

## Files

- .agents/skills/board-workflow/SKILL.md: read, for integrity items 6 and 9 and the picking rule.
- board/README.md: read, for what board/ carries after the migration.
- No file changes. git status on uat stays clean.

## Design

The old mirror''s OWNER-LED contract, and where each part lives now:

| The old guarantee (reader.mjs, deleted in 4c413365) | Carrier today | Read by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| OWNER-LED mapped to a visible Owner-led column | the Awaiting Owner column, id 9, stateType started, renamed in 3d500a0f | get_project columns |
| an awaitingOwner row rendered its waiting-on line | the Waiting on custom field, id 3, set in the same breath as the move (integrity item 6) | get_issue fields, SKILL.md item 6 |
| an OWNER-LED row never counted as done | the picking rule: only Backlog and Ready are runnable, Done alone is completed | SKILL.md picking rule |
| parked never reads active, active never parked | integrity item 9 | SKILL.md item 9 |
| the badge and need-your-decision affordance | the itsaplan product''s own column and card UI, owner-approved in T-144 | the board at localhost:3001 |

The invariant: a row awaiting the owner sits in a started, never-completed column, carries its
question in Waiting on, and is never picked.

Alternatives rejected: patching the product (upstream UI, owner-visible change, needs an owner
order); rebuilding the mirror (undoes the owner-approved migration); duplicating the discipline into
board/README.md (a second home for one rule goes stale; the skill is the pinned home).

## Steps

1. Verify the carriers on the live board and write the measured evidence into this doc''s Proof
   section. 1 point.
2. Close: the closing comment names what replaced the mirror and what a reader can trust, both
   subtasks ticked, the ticket in Done. 1 point.

Total: 2 points.

## Risks

- A future reader wants the old mirror back. This doc names the commits that removed it and the
  owner order a rebuild would need.
- The product changes its column semantics upstream. The board is pinned by VERSION in board/env;
  an upgrade re-runs this verification.

## Proof

Each probe, with its measured result from 2026-10-19:

- get_project on T returns the column { id: 9, name: "Awaiting Owner", stateType: "started", position: 6 }. Measured: exact match.
- list_custom_fields on T returns { id: 3, name: "Waiting on", fieldType: "text" }. Measured: exact match.
- grep -n on .agents/skills/board-workflow/SKILL.md: line 150 carries integrity item 6, line 153 carries item 9, line 48 carries the picking rule (Backlog or Ready, and nothing else). Measured: all three found.
- git status --porcelain in the main tree: zero lines, branch uat. Measured: clean, the repository carries no change from this ticket.
- git show --stat 4c413365 lists board/reader.mjs, board/server.mjs, board/index.html and board/tests/reader.test.mjs as deletions. Measured: confirmed. The deleted reader mapped OWNER-LED to the Owner-led column at its lines 23 to 24 and flagged awaitingOwner rows with their waiting-on line at lines 287 to 305, which is the contract the table above maps.
', NULL, 'file-text', '{}', false, false, false, NULL, 14336, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-19 14:04:56.254977+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document_revision (id, document_id, version, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, "position", owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, created_at) VALUES (39, 16, 1, NULL, 'T-240', '# T-240 - Language switch on Android: a black span replaces the loader face for half a second

## Goal

A language switch on Android shows one loader face from the tap to the settled app. The half-second
black span with the white spinner that today follows the loader''s disappearance is gone.

## Root cause, named from source

The cover''s face record never binds the values the JS side sends.

`SwitchCoverFace` (modules/switchcover/android/.../SwitchCoverModule.kt) declares its eight
properties without `@Field`. expo-modules-core''s record conversion drops every property that lacks
that annotation - `RecordTypeConverter.kt`, both the reflection and the introspectable strategy:
`property.findAnnotation<Field>() ?: return@mapNotNull null` - and because no property is
`@Required`, nothing throws. The converter constructs the record through its all-defaults
constructor and resolves the call successfully. The cover therefore paints its defaults on every
show, on every build since the module landed: `backgroundColor #000000`, `spinnerColor #ffffff`,
`lineText ""`. That is precisely the owner''s span: a fully black background, a white spinner, and
no line.

The phone''s timeline: the tap reveals the JS loader (purple, spinner, line); the commit converges
behind it; `showSwitchCover` adds the cover over the activity''s content - in default black;
`Updates.reloadAsync()` tears the React context down, so the purple JS loader vanishes ("after
about half a second the loading face disappears"); the black-defaults cover alone holds the
restart span, about half a second on the 3T; the new context''s first content commit lifts the
cover and the app appears.

Why the earlier pass missed it: the #141 device read that certified the cover ran before the
audit''s history rewrite (bf40b662), and its continuous face is the JS loader plus the purple
window background - the span a non-showing cover leaves. The rewritten module, the one that
ships, never met a device again. 2.0.1131 is the first production build the owner exercised
since, and its cover shows - wrongly.

## The fix

Annotate every property of `SwitchCoverFace` with `@Field` and `@Required`.

- `@Field` binds each JS key to its property, so the cover paints the loader''s own purple,
  spinner colour and size, line text, colour, size and offsets.
- `@Required` turns any future contract drift into a loud refusal: the call rejects,
  `commitLanguagePreference`''s existing catch logs the cover-refused warning and relaunches
  bare onto the purple window background, instead of silently painting a black face. Silent
  defaults are exactly what hid this for two versions.
- The constructor defaults stay: `ObjectConstructorFactory` builds the instance through the
  all-optional constructor before setting fields, and the defaults are unreachable once every
  field is required.

No JS changes: `coverOptionsFor`, the binding and the commit path are untouched, so the
not-switching path is byte-identical and iOS is untouched by construction.

## The guard, red first

`modules/switchcover/__tests__/faceRecord.test.ts`, the source-reading pattern of
`shared/__tests__/versionLockstep.test.ts`: it reads the Kotlin record and the TS
`SwitchCoverOptions` and pins the parse contract - the record implements `Record`, every
property carries `@Field` and `@Required`, and the property names equal the TS type''s keys.
It fails on today''s tree and passes after the fix. The mutation pass strips one annotation and
renames one property and watches the guard redden. Jest cannot compile Kotlin, so the unit
guard pins the contract structurally and the device step witnesses the pixels.

## Steps

1. **Step 1: the cover''s face record binds the loader''s values** (2 points) - the guard suite
   red on uat, the `@Field`/`@Required` annotations, the guard green, the mutation pass,
   `yarn validate`.
2. **Step 2: the 3T shows one loader face across both switch directions** (3 points, device,
   fresh session) - build the branch through build-mock.zsh, drive en to ar and ar to en (and
   one same-record switch for the instant path), record the span, read the frames: no black
   frame with a white spinner between the loader and the settled app, both directions. The
   release-variant build also proves the annotation lookup survives R8. Logcat holds no
   cover-refused warning.

## Alternatives rejected

- Passing a bare Map instead of a Record: the JSI path still has to name the keys, and a
  missing key then renders as null into a colour parse that throws. The Record with required
  fields keeps the contract typed and the refusal loud.
- Defaults set to the loader''s palette instead of `@Required`: a second copy of the palette
  that drifts silently, and a partial parse would still paint a half-right face. The refusal is
  the honest failure.
- Re-showing the cover from the new context: the cover already spans the restart; the bug was
  only ever the values it painted.

## Risks

- Kotlin reflection under R8: stock expo modules parse `@Field` records in release builds
  (expo-updates'' own `ReloadScreenOptions` among them), and the device step runs the
  release-variant mock build, so the witness covers it.
- The record''s constructor defaults remain black: unreachable behind `@Required`, and the
  guard pins the annotations, so removing one reddens the suite.

## Points

Step 1 is 2 (code), Step 2 is 3 (device): 5 on the ticket.', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, 15360, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-19 14:07:10.63516+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document_revision (id, document_id, version, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, "position", owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, created_at) VALUES (40, 16, 2, NULL, 'T-240', '# T-240 - Language switch on Android: a black span replaces the loader face for half a second

## Goal

A language switch on Android shows one loader face from the tap to the settled app. The half-second
black span with the white spinner that today follows the loader''s disappearance is gone.

## Root cause, named from source

The cover''s face record never binds the values the JS side sends.

`SwitchCoverFace` (modules/switchcover/android/.../SwitchCoverModule.kt) declares its eight
properties without `@Field`. expo-modules-core''s record conversion drops every property that lacks
that annotation - `RecordTypeConverter.kt`, both the reflection and the introspectable strategy:
`property.findAnnotation<Field>() ?: return@mapNotNull null` - and because no property is
`@Required`, nothing throws. The converter constructs the record through its all-defaults
constructor and resolves the call successfully. The cover therefore paints its defaults on every
show, on every build since the module landed: `backgroundColor #000000`, `spinnerColor #ffffff`,
`lineText ""`. That is precisely the owner''s span: a fully black background, a white spinner, and
no line.

The phone''s timeline: the tap reveals the JS loader (purple, spinner, line); the commit converges
behind it; `showSwitchCover` adds the cover over the activity''s content - in default black;
`Updates.reloadAsync()` tears the React context down, so the purple JS loader vanishes ("after
about half a second the loading face disappears"); the black-defaults cover alone holds the
restart span, about half a second on the 3T; the new context''s first content commit lifts the
cover and the app appears.

Why the earlier pass missed it: the #141 device read that certified the cover ran before the
audit''s history rewrite (bf40b662), and its continuous face is the JS loader plus the purple
window background - the span a non-showing cover leaves. The rewritten module, the one that
ships, never met a device again. 2.0.1131 is the first production build the owner exercised
since, and its cover shows - wrongly.

## The second defect, found by the first device read (2026-10-19)

With `@Field` binding the values, they finally reach `Color.parseColor` - and the line colour
`''#fff''` is a shorthand that parser rejects outright (it accepts only `#RRGGBB` and `#AARRGGBB`).
The show aborted on every switch, five refusals in the read''s logcat, and the bare-relaunch
fallback hid it: the frames showed no black span, but through the loader holding the span itself,
not through the cover. The fix ships the white expanded (`''#ffffff''`), and the face suite pins
every shipped colour to the two parseable hex forms. The loader''s own `''#fff''` is untouched:
React Native parses shorthand, so its pixels never move.

## The fix

Annotate every property of `SwitchCoverFace` with `@Field` and `@Required`.

- `@Field` binds each JS key to its property, so the cover paints the loader''s own purple,
  spinner colour and size, line text, colour, size and offsets.
- `@Required` turns any future contract drift into a loud refusal: the call rejects,
  `commitLanguagePreference`''s existing catch logs the cover-refused warning and relaunches
  bare onto the purple window background, instead of silently painting a black face. Silent
  defaults are exactly what hid this for two versions.
- The constructor defaults stay: `ObjectConstructorFactory` builds the instance through the
  all-optional constructor before setting fields, and the defaults are unreachable once every
  field is required.

No JS changes beside the line colour''s expansion: `coverOptionsFor`''s shape, the binding and the
commit path are untouched, so the not-switching path is byte-identical and iOS is untouched by
construction.

## The guard, red first

`modules/switchcover/__tests__/faceRecord.test.ts`, the source-reading pattern of
`shared/__tests__/versionLockstep.test.ts`: it reads the Kotlin record and the TS
`SwitchCoverOptions` and pins the parse contract - the record implements `Record`, every
property carries `@Field` and `@Required`, and the property names equal the TS type''s keys.
It fails on today''s tree and passes after the fix. The mutation pass strips one annotation and
renames one property and watches the guard redden. Jest cannot compile Kotlin, so the unit
guard pins the contract structurally and the device step witnesses the pixels. The face suite
(`stores/__tests__/languageReloadFace.test.ts`) pins the expanded white and the two parseable
hex forms on every shipped colour, red against the shorthand.

## Steps

1. **Step 1: the cover''s face record binds the loader''s values** (2 points) - the guard suite
   red on uat, the `@Field`/`@Required` annotations, the guard green, the mutation pass,
   `yarn validate`. Landed in 2.0.1137.
2. **Step 2: the 3T shows one loader face across both switch directions** (3 points, device,
   fresh session) - build the branch through build-mock.zsh, drive en to ar and ar to en (and
   one same-record switch for the instant path), record the span, read the frames: no black
   frame with a white spinner between the loader and the settled app, both directions, and no
   cover-refused warning in logcat. The release-variant build also proves the annotation lookup
   survives R8. The first run found the shorthand defect and failed on the warning; it re-runs
   on the branch carrying Step 3.
3. **Step 3: the cover''s shipped line colour is Android-parseable** (2 points) - the face suite
   red against the shorthand, the white expanded at the call site, the parse-safety assertion on
   every shipped colour, the mutation pass, `yarn validate`. Landed in 2.0.1138.

## Alternatives rejected

- Passing a bare Map instead of a Record: the JSI path still has to name the keys, and a
  missing key then renders as null into a colour parse that throws. The Record with required
  fields keeps the contract typed and the refusal loud.
- Defaults set to the loader''s palette instead of `@Required`: a second copy of the palette
  that drifts silently, and a partial parse would still paint a half-right face. The refusal is
  the honest failure.
- Re-showing the cover from the new context: the cover already spans the restart; the bug was
  only ever the values it painted.
- Normalising shorthand hex in the Kotlin module: native code to forgive one lazy literal at one
  call site; the palette''s owner is the JS side, so the expansion lives there.

## Risks

- Kotlin reflection under R8: stock expo modules parse `@Field` records in release builds
  (expo-updates'' own `ReloadScreenOptions` among them), and the device step runs the
  release-variant mock build, so the witness covers it.
- The record''s constructor defaults remain black: unreachable behind `@Required`, and the
  guard pins the annotations, so removing one reddens the suite.
- A future colour added to the face in shorthand: the face suite''s parse-safety assertion
  reddens on the first run.

## Points

Step 1 is 2 (code), Step 2 is 3 (device), Step 3 is 2 (code): 7 on the ticket.', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, 15360, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-19 15:25:49.744677+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document_revision (id, document_id, version, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, "position", owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, created_at) VALUES (41, 13, 8, NULL, 'T-142', '# T-142

## Goal

One new question in the help modal about widget support, answered in one short sentence. Nothing anywhere teaches how to add a widget.

## Scope ruling (owner, 2026-10-19)

The owner scrapped the original scope whole: no Widgets row in Settings, no OS gallery surface, no Android pin module, no how-to card. The investigation record (comment 1979) stands as history; none of its three options is built. What remains is the question-only feature below, ordered as basic as possible.

## Decisions

- D142.6 (2026-10-19, owner): scrap the Widgets row and every surface the investigation offered. Why: the owner does not want to teach users how to add widgets, and neither platform exposes an open-gallery API.
- D142.7 (2026-10-19): the question is one HELP_ENTRIES entry appended after `help.q.cutoff`, with no steps array and no action. Why: the entries are ordered by how often each is the cause, a support question is not a cause, so it goes last, and `ModalHelp` then needs no code change.
- D142.8 (2026-10-19): draft copy pinned by the test - question "Are widgets supported?", answer one sentence naming where the widgets appear and nothing else. Why: the owner''s order is a compact question and a compact answer only.
- D142.9 (2026-10-19): the help suite''s owner-cut test is rewritten, not deleted: its /widget/i ban dates from the earlier cut and the owner''s 2026-10-19 order supersedes it, while the "How far ahead" ban stays. Why: the suite can never go green with the ban standing, and deleting the test would drop the surviving cut''s guard.
- D142.10 (2026-10-19): the answer is one key per platform, grounded in what ships - iOS "Yes, on the home screen and the lock screen." (PrayerWidget home sizes plus the PrayerLockWidget and ExtrasLockWidget kinds), Android "Yes, on the home screen." (plugins/androidWidgetGrid.js; Android has no lock-screen widgets). Why: D142.8 names where the widgets appear, and the two platforms do not appear in the same places.

## Files

Changed:
- `shared/help.ts` - one entry appended last, with its own one-line comment (a support question, not a cause).
- `shared/i18n/en.ts` - three keys after `help.a.cutoff`: `help.q.widgets`, `help.a.widgets.ios`, `help.a.widgets.android`.
- `shared/i18n/ar.ts`, `ms.ts`, `so.ts`, `hi.ts`, `th.ts` - the same three keys each, translated (copy in Design).
- `shared/i18n/catalog-lock.json` - re-taken with `yarn i18n:lock` in the same commit.
- `shared/__tests__/help.test.ts` - the new assertions land red first; the owner-cut test rewritten per D142.9.
- `package.json`, `app.json` - the version bump (the worktree carries no `android/`; it is gitignored).

Read, no change: `components/modals/Help.tsx` (the Topic row renders steps and action only when present), `scripts/catalog-lock.mjs`, `shared/__tests__/catalogGates.test.ts`, `shared/__tests__/catalogLock.test.ts`, `components/modals/__tests__/Help.test.tsx` (iterates getHelpTopics, so it adapts itself).

## Design

The entry mirrors the volume question''s shape: one shared question key, two platform answer keys, no steps, no action.

English copy, pinned:
- `help.q.widgets`: "Are widgets supported?"
- `help.a.widgets.ios`: "Yes, on the home screen and the lock screen."
- `help.a.widgets.android`: "Yes, on the home screen."

Translated copy (question, ios answer, android answer):
- ar: هل الأدوات مدعومة؟ / نعم، على الشاشة الرئيسية وشاشة القفل. / نعم، على الشاشة الرئيسية.
- ms: Adakah widget disokong? / Ya, pada skrin utama dan skrin kunci. / Ya, pada skrin utama.
- so: Ma widget-na waa la taageerayaa? / Haa, shaashadda bilowga iyo shaashadda xiritaanka. / Haa, shaashadda bilowga.
- hi: क्या विजेट समर्थित हैं? / हाँ, होम स्क्रीन और लॉक स्क्रीन पर। / हाँ, होम स्क्रीन पर।
- th: รองรับวิดเจ็ตหรือไม่? / ใช่ บนหน้าจอหลักและหน้าจอล็อก / ใช่ บนหน้าจอหลัก

The catalog gates bound every translation: the exact en key set, no Athan brand bytes, no English echo, no Latin script in ar, and the length budget. The lock goes stale the moment en gains keys, so `yarn i18n:lock` runs in the same commit, before `yarn validate`.

The new tests: the widget topic lands last with no steps and no action on both platforms; the English copy is pinned byte for byte; every locale''s three keys carry no instruction vocabulary (per-locale word lists, so the ban is observable in non-Latin scripts too); each English string is one sentence. The mutation pass removes the entry, moves it off last, adds steps to it, edits the pinned copy, and plants an instruction word in one translation: each mutation must turn the suite red.

Alternatives rejected: one shared answer key (the platforms do not appear in the same places, and the volume entry sets the two-key precedent); reusing the What''s New widget vocabulary (it echoes English untranslated in every locale under an echo exemption, so it names no screens in translation); any position but last (D142.7).

## Steps

1. (code, weight 2) The entry and its copy: `help.q.widgets` with one answer key per platform in `shared/help.ts`, the six catalogues, `yarn i18n:lock` re-taken, red-then-green help coverage.
2. (device, weight 3) The device read: atlas first (`e2e/device-atlas-oneplus3t.md`, `e2e/device-atlas-iphone-xs.md`), the help modal opened on the 3T and the owner''s iPhone, the new question transcribed, one comment each.

## Story points

5 = 2 + 3, one number in two places: the ticket''s estimate and the steps'' estimates.

## Risks

- The rewritten cut-ban could read as reversing an owner cut. D142.6 and D142.9 record the 2026-10-19 order that supersedes it, and the "How far ahead" ban stays guarded.
- A translation that echoes its English fails the echo gate. The pinned translations differ in every locale, and the non-Latin scripts carry no Latin bytes for the ar gate.
- The catalog lock goes stale the moment en changes. `yarn i18n:lock` is re-taken in the same commit, before validate.
- The answer could drift into instruction in a later edit. The new tests pin the exact copy, assert no steps and no action, and sweep instruction vocabulary in all six locales.

## Proof

The help test red before the entry exists and green after it (`yarn jest shared/__tests__/help.test.ts`), `yarn validate` green with the catalog lock test included, and both device reads as comments on the ticket.
', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, 12288, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-19 15:31:54.194976+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document_revision (id, document_id, version, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, "position", owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, created_at) VALUES (22, 9, 2, NULL, 'T-140', '# T-140: Arabic line height on Android, the gaps the owner sees on glass

## Goal

The owner compared the phones under Arabic and named three gap sites where the 3T sits taller than the iPhone: the Arabic prayer name above the countdown against the countdown, the location against the date on both schedules, and the bottom sheet''s title against its subheading. Wording, lettering and clipping are correct everywhere; only spacing differs. Row 137 established the standing explanation: the same Rubik TTF renders a taller effective line box on Android (nearest the head bbox set, measured 1.578 to 1.633em with a +2 view-bound residual) while the iPhone sits on hhea/typo (1.185em exact). This ticket measures each named site''s rendered line box and inter-element gap on both phones, attributes each gap to the platform line box or to the layout, and brings the two options with exact numbers and blast radius. The ticket ends Owner-led: the owner rules between accepting the platform box (row 87''s law) and pinning an ar lineHeight per surface family (no negative pulling). No code, no pixel change, no install.

## Decisions taken (agent-ruled 2026-10-19)

- **D140.1 Vehicles, read-only.** Drive the installed builds: 3T `com.example.athan` 2.0.908, XS `com.example.athan` 2.0.939. Why: the full geometry-bearing diff from both vehicle commits (9dd4eb6a, 6446955f) to uat head over `components/`, `shared/`, `app/` and `assets/fonts/` touches none of the three sites'' geometry - the only component change is Countdown''s UNIT_SIZE_SHARE 0.9 to 0.95 (a unit-letter font size inside the digits row that moves no margin and no line box; the row''s line box is set by the 24pt digits), and the Help, ColorPicker and Settings changes are row 136''s chevron direction pins. The fonts are unchanged. A fresh 2.0.1108 build would read the same numbers at the cost of installing on the owner''s phones.
- **D140.2 Site 1 authority.** The prayer-name node above the countdown is a standing full-width band artefact on both phones (row 137, void on both). The site-1 gap is delivered from ink: the digits'' nodes corroborate, the pixels measure. Ink gaps are the deliverable at every site; node bounds corroborate.
- **D140.3 Site 3 on the XS.** The settings sheet publishes zero accessibility content nodes on the XS (row 137''s void). Pixels are the XS authority for the sheet pair; the 3T''s node bounds corroborate the margin.
- **D140.4 Controls are repeatability.** Row 137''s pin controls proved both instruments (3T Help pair, overlay body on both). This ticket''s control is the re-read: every surface in each step''s control list (step 2: 3T location 67px, date 75px, sheet title 93px, subtitle 58px; step 3: XS location 57px, date 64px, countdown word 61px, digits container 86px) must reproduce within 1px, or the step stops and records the drift.
- **D140.5 Scope of both schedules.** The location-date pair is read on the Standard and the Extras page on both phones; the countdown pair on both pages; the sheet pair on the Settings sheet, the app''s bottom sheet.
- **D140.6 No repo commits.** The record lives on the board. The font-tables script runs from git history (6ff7364d) restored into session scratch, never committed; screenshots never enter git.
- **D140.7 Not-ar arrival (grill finding 9).** If either phone arrives with the app language not ar, the executor records the found state, sets the app''s own language to العربية (an in-app preference, not an OS setting), measures, and restores the found language afterward. The invariant "no OS settings change" stands; the app language is app state, and the record carries the restore.

## Files (read only)

- `components/countdown/Countdown.tsx` - site 1: name 17 Rubik-Regular, marginBottom 3dp, translateY -2; digits 24 Rubik-Medium in the timer row.
- `components/day/Day.tsx` - site 2: location 16 Rubik-Regular with marginBottom 8dp under ar; date 18 Rubik-Regular.
- `components/sheets/parts/Header.tsx` - site 3: title 22 Rubik-Medium with marginBottom 0 under ar; subtitle 14 Rubik-Regular with marginTop 6dp under ar.
- `shared/typography.ts` - the ar record: sizes, faces, the scriptLineHeight pin pattern (round(n x 1.5)).
- `shared/constants.ts` - SPACING (xxs 2, quart 5, py 6, sm 8), TEXT sizes (14, 16, 18, 26).
- `assets/fonts/Rubik-Regular.ttf`, `assets/fonts/Rubik-Medium.ttf` - the metric table, via the row-137 script from history.
- `e2e/device-atlas-oneplus3t.md`, `e2e/device-atlas-iphone-xs.md` - driving routes and instrument notes.
- Row 137''s record in git history: 6ff7364d (expectations), da1b0b7b and f1b7428e (3T), 896fd998 and 4431aaa1 (XS), 9de7215d (adjudication), 4b6dfb72 (verdict and options).

## Design

Row 137''s ladder, extended one rung to the gaps between elements:

1. **Expectation and margin table.** Candidate line boxes per face and size from the shipped TTFs at both scales (3T 2.625, XS 3.0), each set''s ascent and descent (baseline position inside each candidate box), and the code''s margin per site in dp, 3T px and XS pt, with the expected node gap per site: site 1 name marginBottom 3dp (the translateY -2 shifts the name''s own box and ink up inside its margin, expected node gap 3dp, ink gap 3dp minus 2 shifted), site 2 location marginBottom 8dp, site 3 title 0 plus subtitle marginTop 6dp. The margins are platform-independent (dp), so any cross-platform node-gap delta above the margin is already suspect.
2. **Node bounds.** The live accessibility tree per phone: 3T through `maestro hierarchy` (`uiautomator dump` stays refused on Android 9), XS through agent-device snapshot frames. Two independent captures per screen must agree on every named site''s bounds and every static label, byte for byte; the countdown digits node is compared on bounds only, because its accessibility label reticks every second.
3. **Ink bounds, the row-scan contract (per site, per phone).** The executor''s script (PIL, in session scratch) processes one screenshot per screen. Region: the rectangle spanning the site''s two elements, sourced from the published node bounds where they exist; for a void surface the region is anchored on the adjacent published bound extended by the code margin plus the candidate box height (site 1: the digits node extended upward by 3dp margin plus the 17pt name''s candidate box; site 3 on the XS: the sheet chrome bounds from the snapshot tree plus the header''s paddingTop, spanning title and subtitle). Predicate: convert to grayscale; per row, background = the row''s median luminance inside the region; a row is ink-candidate when row median minus row minimum exceeds 80/255 (strong) and is flagged weak between 25/255 and 80/255 (the text shadow is a 0.3-alpha black halo offset 3 down with radius 6, which reads weak). Band rule: consecutive ink-candidate rows merge into one band across gaps under 3 failing rows; bands under 6px tall are flagged as fragments, never dropped. Output: the two-threshold band table per region. The vision worker then names each band (which is the name, the digits, the location, the date, the title, the subtitle, which is shadow or gradient artefact) from the same crop, and the record carries the script bands and the vision names side by side. A band the two rungs disagree on becomes a recorded void, not a guess. Spent digit groups do not render in either installed build (Countdown filters them), so no dimming confound exists.
4. **The gap table.** Per site per phone: node gap (box to box) where published, ink gap (what the owner sees), code margin, and the overhang split - the space above and below the ink inside each adjacent box, measured from ink position inside node bounds where both exist. Predicted overhang cross-check: box top to baseline per metric set is the set''s ascent times size times scale; predicted ink top = baseline minus the measured ink height above baseline, so each platform''s matched set (3T the bbox set with the +2 residual, XS hhea) must predict the measured overhang within 2px.
5. **Attribution.** Two branches, both stated per site. Where the node gap is readable on both phones: the gap difference is the platform line box''s when the node gap equals the code margin on both phones and the Android-minus-iPhone ink-gap delta closes against the overhang deltas within 2px; it is the layout''s when a node gap itself departs from the code margin. Where a node gap is void (site 1 both phones, site 3 on the XS): the margin-plus-box model stands in - the expected ink gap is computed from code margin plus each platform''s predicted overhangs under its matched set, and the measured ink gap attributes to the platform line box when it matches the 3T-set prediction and not the XS-set prediction at the same margin. Each site gets one line naming the branch and the deciding numbers.
6. **The options.** (a) Accept the platform box: each site''s measured cross-platform ink-gap delta, row 87''s law unchanged. (b) Pin an ar lineHeight per surface family, the pin set enumerated per node: name 17 to 20, location 16 to 19, date 18 to 21, title 22 to 26, subtitle 14 to 17 (each round(1.185 x size), the hhea parity the XS already renders), with the digits row (24 Rubik-Medium, Latin digits) carried as a ruled-in-or-out variant: a 24 to 28 pin on the digits side only if the owner wants that side compressed too, both variants priced with numbers. The floor per pin: the measured ink height of that surface''s rendered strings on the 3T, plus 1px; a pin below its floor risks clipping (the ar clamped-blocks precedent in typography.ts) and is not offered - this is the no-negative-pulling rule made numeric. The residual gap each pin leaves (the ink gap after the pin, predicted and stated), and the blast radius: component-scoped (three files, the named surfaces only) against record-scoped (every ar consumer of that face and size, named from step 1''s sweep).

Invariant: no code, no pixel change, no install, no OS settings change; read-only contact; the phones restored as found (language restored per D140.7 if it was touched); screenshots stay in `$HOME/athan-gitree/sessions/140/` and never enter git.

Alternatives rejected:

- Building and installing 2.0.1108 on both phones: rejected under D140.1, identical geometry, a device mutation for no measurement delta.
- Deriving the ink gaps arithmetically from the metric sets without pixels: rejected. The deliverable is what the owner sees on glass; ink is measured, then reconciled against the metric arithmetic as a cross-check.
- Reading only the Standard schedule: rejected, the owner named both schedules (D140.5).

## Steps

1. **Step 1: The expectation, margin and sweep table (weight 1, docs).** Restore the row-137 tables script from 6ff7364d into session scratch, extend SIZES to 14, 16, 17, 18, 22, 24 and extend its columns to print each set''s ascent and descent (hhea, typo, win ascent and descent; head yMax and yMin) so the baseline position inside every candidate box exists; run it against the shipped TTFs. Read the three components'' margins into the expected-gap table (dp, 3T px, XS pt). Sweep every consumer of the measured faces and sizes under ar: `grep -rn "TEXT.sizeSmall\|TEXT.size)\|ty.size(22)\|countdownSize" components/` read down to the rendered face and size per hit, producing the blast-radius table step 5 cites. Proof: the shared sizes (14, 22) reproduce row 137''s EXPECTATIONS.md line-height numbers byte for byte.
2. **Step 2: The 3T readings (weight 3, device, runs alone).** Read-only on `com.example.athan` 2.0.908. Pre-flight vehicle line: `wm size` (1080x1920), `wm density` (420), `settings get system font_scale` (1.0 expected; a different value stops the step as drift). Verify app language ar on arrival (D140.7 otherwise). `maestro hierarchy` twice per screen on Standard home, Extras home and the settings sheet (day labels, countdown name and digits nodes, sheet title and subtitle); screencap each screen; repeatability controls against row 137 (location 67px, date 75px, sheet title 93px, subtitle 58px, each within 1px); record bounds verbatim with the vehicle line; restore as found.
3. **Step 3: The XS readings (weight 3, device, runs alone).** Read-only on `com.example.athan` 2.0.939. Pre-flight vehicle line: the home face matches row 137''s layout (location and date present at the top of the list, settings button bottom-centre); the panel is 375 x 812 pt at 3x. Verify app language ar on arrival (D140.7 otherwise). agent-device snapshot frames on Standard and Extras (day labels, countdown digits container), pymobiledevice3 screenshots of both home pages and the settings sheet (the standing AX void makes pixels the sheet authority); repeatability controls (location 57px, date 64px, countdown word 61px, digits container 86px, each within 1px); restore as found.
4. **Step 4: The vision ink reads (weight 1, docs).** Run the row-scan contract''s script per screenshot, then dispatch the vision worker per screenshot with the band table and the crop: name and bound the ink at every site on both phones. Proof: every named ink band sits inside a node bound where both exist (ink under box, row 137''s relation), and every disagreement becomes a recorded void.
5. **Step 5: The attribution and options record (weight 1, docs).** The measured table (each cell the instrument publishes, a named void in the row-120 form where it does not; a site whose ink read voids on one phone stands on the other phone''s read plus the matched-set prediction, and the record names the void), the attribution line per site, the two options with exact numbers and blast radius citing step 1''s sweep, the closing OWNER-LED move with Waiting on set and the two-line recommendation.

## Risks

- The countdown name''s node stays a band artefact: covered by D140.2, ink is the deliverable and row 137 corroborates the rendered word is normal on both phones.
- The XS sheet publishes no nodes again: covered by D140.3, pixels are the standing authority.
- A repeatability control fails: the step stops and records both readings as instrument drift; the ticket reports the confound rather than guessing.
- The mock API names a different prayer or date than row 137''s session: irrelevant to geometry, the record names the word each bound was read under.
- Text shadows and the gradient background blur the row scan: the two-threshold band table plus the vision worker''s naming adjudicates; a disagreement becomes a recorded void, not a guess.
- The day rolls mid-capture (mock evening window): both pages of one phone are captured back to back; a date-string change between captures shows as a repeatability control failure and stops the step.

## Proof

- The plan and record: `list_issue_documents` on T-140.
- Step 1: shared-size rows reproduce row 137''s expectation numbers; the sweep table names every ar consumer of each measured face and size.
- Steps 2 and 3: the two independent captures agree on every named bound and static label; the control lines pass within 1px.
- Step 4: every named ink band sits inside its node bound where both exist.
- Step 5: every option number traces to a table row in this record; the ticket stands Owner-led with Waiting on naming the owner''s ruling; `git diff uat..lane/140` shows no source change.

