## Goal

What is wrong today: on the iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1), open pose, the app renders inside a system-scaled centred portrait window, so pure black system letterbox bars flank the purple gradient, and the scaled window delivers a top safe-area inset of about 80pt, which pushes the countdown block about 82pt down the 669pt window until the Standard page''s Isha row overlaps the Settings button by about 46pt. True when done: the owner rules which fix vehicle this row takes, and the ruled fix renders the purple background edge to edge with the content still one centred schedule inside the existing 500pt max content width, the countdown at its designed offset, and Isha clearing the Settings button, with portrait poses and every other form factor unchanged.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-20 The two mechanisms were measured on the booted iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1, SIMULATOR_UDID), driving the installed 2.1.20 Release build (code equal to uat modulo version files), before any change was designed. Why: the row''s first acceptance criterion names the mechanisms from measurement, and the fix choice depends on which layer paints each defect.
- 2026-10-20 Mechanism 1, the bars: a panel-level screenshot (951x669) reads pure black (0,0,0) from x=0 to about x=241 and from about x=709 to x=951, and the app gradient (#031a4c to #5b1eaa) spans exactly x about 241 to 709. The app''s accessibility tree spans 469x669, matching row 116''s close. The bars are the system letterbox around the portrait-locked app''s window: no view the app owns exists there, and no app color is (0,0,0) (the darkest app color is #031a4c = (3,26,76)). Why: this names the painting layer as the system, outside the app''s window.
- 2026-10-20 Mechanism 2, the gap and the push-down: in the open-pose window the countdown''s prayer-name text frame starts at y=95 against y=13 in the closed pose, an 82pt difference on a window 9pt shorter. With the countdown container''s constants (marginTop 6, height 54, centered content of about 70pt) the delivered top safe-area inset works out to about 80pt in the open-pose window against about 0 in the closed pose; app/Screen.tsx paddingTop (insets.top + 17) honors it. The content span from name top to Isha bottom is 519.33pt in both poses, so the inflated top lands Isha''s bottom at y=614.33 against the Settings button top at y=568: a 46.33pt overlap. The closed pose clears by 44.67pt (Isha bottom 532.33, button top 577). Why: the gap keys off the safe-area top inset the scaled window delivers, and one mechanism explains both the gap and the overlap.
- 2026-10-20 The fix frontier, measured and read against Apple''s Duo material recorded on row 116: the purple can run edge to edge only if the app''s window spans the 951x669 inner display, and iOS grants that window only to an app whose supported orientations include landscape ("iPhone Duo respects your supported interface orientations"; a portrait-only app "will scale on the inner display" - Tech Talks 111461, quoted in row 116''s Requirements section). No Apple API paints the system letterbox, and Info.plist orientation arrays carry no per-model scoping (the generated plist holds Portrait and PortraitUpsideDown for both iPhone and iPad). Why: every lawful-seeming fix reduces to one of three vehicles below.
- 2026-10-20 Vehicle A, adding landscape to UISupportedInterfaceOrientations (iPhone array only, Android and iPad untouched): it fills the panel on the Duo, and it lets every physical iPhone rotate, where the layout column (519.33pt of rows plus countdown and header, about 640pt total) does not fit a phone landscape window of about 390-430pt and clips with no scroll. Rendering sanely there needs landscape layout support, which this row''s orders freeze. Breaches the fence (every other form factor unchanged).
- 2026-10-20 Vehicle B, a supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor gate that answers landscape only for the Duo''s panel: phones stay locked and the Duo fills. It branches on device or panel class, which the standing ruling names Duo-branched code and bans; no Apple requirement forces it, so only the owner lifts it.
- 2026-10-20 Vehicle C, keeping Apple''s sanctioned scaled centred window: the bars stay, which the owner''s wanted outcome rejects; the tall gap and the Isha overlap could still be repaired inside the scaled window by capping the honored top inset at a phone-class value, which moves no phone pixel because no phone delivers a top inset above about 62pt.
- 2026-10-20 This plan stops at the frontier and tables the ruling instead of writing any of the three vehicles. Why: the dispatch''s stop rule names both Vehicle A''s need (landscape layout support) and Vehicle B''s shape (Duo-branched code) as stop conditions, and the row''s note ordering landscape rendering on the Duo open pose does not choose between them: it orders the outcome, and the outcome''s only vehicles are the two banned shapes.

## Files

Read:
- app/Screen.tsx (paddingTop: insets.top + SCREEN.paddingTop; maxWidth 500, centred column), app/Navigation.tsx (BackgroundGradients absolute fill at the root; bottom-anchored Settings button), app/_layout.tsx (root background #2c1c77), components/countdown/Countdown.tsx (container marginTop 6, height 54, centered content), components/day/Day.tsx, components/prayer/List.tsx, components/ui/BackgroundGradients.tsx, shared/constants.ts (SCREEN.paddingTop 17, SPACING.xxs 2, SPACING.lg 16, SIZE.contentMaxWidth 500, STYLES.countdown.height 54, COLORS.navigation.background #031a4c, rootBackground #2c1c77, gradient #031a4c to #5b1eaa).
- app.json (orientation portrait, requireFullScreen true) and plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.js; ios/Athan/Info.plist in the main tree (both orientation arrays Portrait and PortraitUpsideDown, UIRequiresFullScreen true).
- Row 116''s plan doc (doc id 20) for the sanctioned geometry, Apple quotes and the SDK ladder; e2e/README.md Gotchas before measuring.

Changed: none. The frontier ruling precedes any change; the ruled vehicle reopens this plan with steps, points and subtasks.

## Design

The deliverable of this pass is the measured mechanism record and the ruled choice. The standing design ruling holds verbatim: one schedule, centred, inside the existing 500pt max content width; no two schedules, no centre settings button, no Duo-branched layout. The app already paints its gradient to its window''s full width (Navigation renders BackgroundGradients as an absolute fill and the content column self-constrains), so a window that spans the panel renders the owner''s wanted outcome with no layout code: purple edge to edge, one centred column.

Alternatives rejected:
- Painting the letterbox purple from inside the app: the bars lie outside the app''s window; no app view or color reaches them (measured above).
- Sizing the window by removing UIRequiresFullScreen: the Duo honors supported orientations regardless; the Tech Talk pair (respects orientations; still resizes on fold) leaves the scaled portrait window in place, which the measurement confirms.
- Any threshold tuned to distinguish the Duo''s panel from a phone landscape (aspect ratios, display metrics): a tuned constant selecting one device is Duo-branched code with extra steps, banned by the same ruling that bans the name.

## Risks

- The owner rules a vehicle this row''s fences forbid without naming the lifting of the fence: the plan re-records the ruling in Decisions before writing the vehicle, so the audit can check the authority.
- The full-display window''s own top inset is unmeasured (it exists only after a ruled build): the plan''s steps for the ruled vehicle must re-measure the countdown offset and the Isha clearance in the granted window, because about 80pt was the scaled window''s inset and the full window may deliver a different one. If the full window still pushes Isha onto the button, the fix adds a top-inset cap keyed to bind only where no phone geometry binds (no phone delivers a top inset above about 62pt), red-tested first.
- The simulator''s hinge-angle read returns no sample (three attempts this session, matching row 116''s instrument note): every pose claim is confirmed by the lit panel''s viewport and frame dimensions instead.

## Proof

- Mechanism 1: the panel screenshot''s pixel columns (black 0,0,0 at x=0, 60, 120, 200, 240 and x=750, 830, 900, 950; gradient at x=300 to 650) beside the app tree''s 469x669 extent. Screenshot held in scratch outside the repository, never committed.
- Mechanism 2: the accessibility frames quoted in Decisions (name text y=95 open against y=13 closed; Isha bottom 614.33 against Settings top 568 open; 532.33 against 577 closed), transcribed from the two snapshot captures.
- The frontier: the generated Info.plist''s orientation arrays plus Apple''s quoted Duo behavior (row 116''s Requirements section) establish that only supported landscape orientations grant the full-display window.
- The ruling: this doc''s Decisions records the owner''s answer when it lands, and the steps, points and subtasks follow it.
