2. Step 2: the ruled outcome measured on the simulators (device, 3 points). Runs alone in a fresh executor session. Read e2e/README.md''s Gotchas first. Version bump first (patch), then build from the branch: npx expo run:ios -d SIMULATOR_UDID --configuration Release. On the iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1): open pose, landscape - the window spans 951x669, the gradient reads edge to edge with no pure-black side bars, the countdown and schedule centred in the 500pt column; open pose, portrait hold - the 669x951 window class, layout centred, the delivered top and bottom insets transcribed; half-open pose - the same spanning window class as open, no scaled window, no phantom inset; the clamp verdict recorded across all three open-pose readings (identity, or the Decision 5 park); closed pose - a rotation attempt refused, the window in today''s 466x678 class, rendering equal to today; an open-closed-open round trip - the process stays live and each pose renders its own geometry. On the phone-class simulator (iPhone 18 Pro class, iOS 27.0): rotation attempts in both directions refuse; the snapshot stays portrait. One spanning-landscape screenshot attached to this row from scratch outside the worktree, never committed; SIMULATOR_UDID placeholder in every committed record. Snapshot, one commit.
3. Step 2b: the live-unfold re-span lands or is recorded impossible (code, 2 points). Step 2''s measurement found that a process launched folded keeps the 469x669 letterbox on live unfold until relaunch: UIKit does not re-ask the supported orientations when the window changes panels, so the spanning only arrives at the next launch. The ruling''s own words cover this path ("to remove the black letterbox bars"), and this step is Risk 1''s fix branch executed on the open side. Extend plugins/duoLandscapeGate.js with an exported pure editSceneDelegate that inserts into SceneDelegate an override of the iOS-13 UIWindowSceneDelegate callback windowScene(_:didUpdate:interfaceOrientation:traitCollection:) - the callback UIKit fires when the scene''s coordinate space changes, which a panel change is - whose body calls window?.rootViewController?.setNeedsUpdateOfSupportedInterfaceOrientations() (Apple: "Notifies the view controller about a change in supported interface orientations"). Tests red-first, mirroring the AppDelegate suite (insertion once, idempotence, marker throw, the literal selector pin). Version bump, prebuild, rebuild, then measure only the unfold-live path: launch closed, unfold, read the window class and the panel-edge pixels; re-verify the closed refusal and the fresh-open spanning beside it. If the letterbox persists, the same callback body gains the bounded fallback - requestGeometryUpdate(.iOS(interfaceOrientations:)) with the four, called only when the window scene''s screen is the Duo inner display class - and the unfold-live path is measured once more. If both attempts fail, the SceneDelegate insertion is reverted (the AppDelegate gate stays), and the limitation is recorded on the row with the attempt''s evidence. Comment, snapshot, one commit.
4. Step 3: the row''s records closed (docs, 1 point). Read the branch diff and record that it names exactly the plugin, its test, app.json, the version files and board/snapshot/board.sql, plus the inset files when the owner-ordered exemption ran, so no phone-tuned layout value moved; tick the subtasks; regenerate the snapshot. Version bump, snapshot, one commit.

- Live unfold re-spans without a relaunch, or the limitation is recorded: Step 2b''s transcribed unfold-live reading on this row.

## Risks

- UIKit may not re-ask the mask when the fold closes: the closed pose would keep landscape until the next ask. Shows as the closed pose accepting rotation after the round trip in Step 2. Fix branch: the SceneDelegate observes the scene''s screen change and calls setNeedsUpdateOfSupportedInterfaceOrientations on the root view controller.
- The delegate may be asked with a nil window early in launch: the gate fails closed (portrait pair); the worst case on the open Duo is a portrait presentation until the next ask. Shows as a letterboxed or portrait launch frame in Step 2. Fix branch: key on the first connected UIWindowScene''s screen when the window is nil.
- An open-pose top inset above 62: the row parks in Awaiting Owner per Decision 5 instead of landing a clamp change; the spanning background still ships, only the inset honoring waits on the owner.
- A future SDK may change the AppDelegate template: the marker edit throws at prebuild, never silently no-ops, and the unit tests pin the marker against the current template shape.
- Resizable-window contexts (iPhone Mirroring, iPad resizing) treat orientations as a preference on iOS 27+ (Expo SDK 58 docs): outside the verified fence, which covers full-screen phones, the Duo''s poses and full-screen iPads. Recorded, not chased.
- The panel class constant is exact points: a future Duo hardware revision with a different inner panel fails closed to portrait. Recorded, not chased; the constant is named for exactly this read.

## Proof

- Gate named and implemented: Step 1''s prebuild read (the gate in AppDelegate.swift, the four in the iPhone array, ~ipad still portrait-only) and the test suite pinning masks, constants, idempotence and wiring.
- Duo open landscape spans, no bars, centred, transcribed: Step 2''s transcribed readings on this row, plus the attached screenshot.
- Closed pose stays portrait: Step 2''s transcribed refusal and window class on this row.
- No physical iPhone rotates, gate unit-tested: Step 2''s transcribed rotation refusals on the phone-class simulator; the plugin test suite.
- Otherwise unchanged, validate green: Step 3''s recorded branch diff naming no layout value; the phone simulator''s portrait snapshot equal to today''s; yarn validate green at every step commit.


## Audit (2026-10-20)

Both reviews on uat..lane/271 came back sound (comments 2593 plan-conformance, 2597 blind); each re-ran the suites green in the worktree. Then the owner''s final ruling (comment 2585, 05:17) landed between execution''s close and the reviews'' dispatch: Option A is cancelled completely, the landscape-span gate never merges, uat carries none of it, and the Duo''s answer is Option B on row 280 (portrait-tall, rendered top-down, background edge to edge). Row 280''s plan already records the disposition ("Option A documented in one line: cancelled by the owner''s final ruling, never merged").

Findings adjudicated, all moot on a dead branch and recorded for Option B''s builder: the dead ALL_ORIENTATIONS export and the unmet README rule (no user-facing feature ships); the duplicate JS/Swift comment; the sentinel''s fail-open on template drift (key any future sentinel on the gate''s own helper signature, not the override selector); the super bypass of Expo''s subscriber manager. The conformance reviewer''s commit list corrected: the branch carried four commits - 767bb1d9 2.1.34 (the gate), aceab2fc 2.1.35 (the measurements), 825e832f 2.1.36 (Step 2b closed with the limitation), b8d1a358 2.1.37 (the records) - all deleted with the branch by the ruling, none merged.

What the dead branch proved and row 280 inherits: the spanning window arrives the moment the plist declares landscape and the delegate gate keys the 951x669 class (fresh-open launches span, edge to edge, centred column, measured); the closed pose, phones and iPads stay portrait under the gate; the spanning window delivers no phantom inset (clamp identity); and iOS refuses both re-ask routes for a live unfold (the setNeedsUpdate nudge and the class-bounded requestGeometryUpdate fallback), so a process launched folded keeps today''s letterbox until relaunch - a constraint Option B''s portrait-tall canvas does not meet, because a portrait window never letterboxes on that panel.', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, 22528, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-20 05:27:53.714838+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document_revision (id, document_id, version, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, "position", owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, created_at) VALUES (70, 25, 1, NULL, 'T-280', '# T-280

## Goal

What is wrong today: on the iPhone Duo''s open inner display the app never renders on a tall canvas. A portrait-locked launch gets the system-scaled centred 469x669 compat window on the 951x669pt panel with pure-black bars either side (row 264''s measured record), and Option A''s landscape spanning was cancelled by the owner''s final ruling before any of it merged. The owner''s pick is Option B: the open Duo held rotated 90 degrees, the inner display a tall 669x951 canvas, the two panels stacked as top half and bottom half across a horizontal fold seam, no left and right, the app''s existing portrait layout rendering top-down, background edge to edge, no letterbox.

True when this is done: the tall hold renders the app portrait on the full tall canvas edge to edge with no letterbox, the Duo''s closed pose stays portrait, no other device rotates, phones render unchanged, and Option A''s branch never merges. If Apple''s OS refuses the portrait-tall presentation, the row sits in Blocked carrying the documented refusal and the transcribed readings, with no workaround attempted.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-20 D1 The mechanism is Apple''s pose-driven presentation, named from the docs; no API requests the tall canvas. Apple''s Tech Talks 111461 (Prepare your app for iPhone Duo): "Interface orientation on the inner display behaves differently. The inner display doesn''t honor your supported interface orientations." WWDC26 session 278 (Modernize your UIKit app): "In iOS 27, an app''s supported interface orientation is a preference provided to the system. It will be ignored when your app is running in a resizable environment," and UIRequiresFullScreen "no longer opts your app fully out of resizing. Instead, it enables discrete resizing that honors your supported interface orientations." The prepare page (developer.apple.com/iphone-duo/prepare/): fixed orientations and hardcoded screen sizes are the breaking patterns, and an iOS 27 SDK-current app "resizes dynamically on iPhone Duo." The tall canvas arrives as a device pose, produced by rotating the device, never by code. Challenged: UIWindowScene requestGeometryUpdate(.iOS(interfaceOrientations: .portrait)) asks the system directly - rejected because row 271 measured that ask "received yet refused" on this panel, and the Tech Talks line names the inner display as ignoring orientation preferences; an ask the OS refuses is not a path, and forcing it is the hack the owner banned. Committed: the plist carries the maximum, the pose carries the canvas.
- 2026-10-20 D2 Measure before writing code. Row 264 measured the compat letterbox in the landscape hold only; the tall hold was never produced (row 271''s closing revision records the portrait-hold read as sitting "beyond the simulator''s instruments"). Whether today''s portrait-only build already spans tall in the tall hold is unknown, and it decides whether any code lands: if the OS presents the tall canvas to today''s build, Option B is the OS''s own presentation of the shipped app and nothing merges. Challenged: always landing the orientation opt-in would produce evidence one session sooner - rejected because it risks landing a landscape-hold change the owner cancelled and spends a code step that may implement nothing. Committed: Step 1 measures today''s build first, and Steps 2 and 3 carry exact no-op records when it grants the tall canvas.
- 2026-10-20 D3 The code shape, if code lands: a prebuild plugin in the pattern row 271 established, named plugins/duoTallCanvas.js. withInfoPlist sets UISupportedInterfaceOrientations to the four-orientation maximum - row 264''s measured mechanism: the spanning window is granted at launch only to an app that declares the panel''s pose orientation. withAppDelegate inserts an override of application(_:supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor:) returning the portrait pair for every window, with no Duo keying anywhere in the gate. app.json keeps "orientation": "portrait" so Android''s screenOrientation never moves (row 271''s Decision 2 pattern; SDK 58 maps "default" to android:screenOrientation="unspecified"). Challenged: keying all-four on the 951x669 panel class exactly as row 271 did - rejected because that keying exists to present landscape on the Duo, the rendering the owner cancelled; the keying-free portrait pair keeps every screen''s effective set byte-equal to today''s portrait-only plist, so whatever the inner display presents is the OS''s per-pose choice, not a branch this repo chose. Committed: keying-free.
- 2026-10-20 D4 The landscape hold is transcribed, never engineered. If the plist maximum lands, the landscape hold may present spanning landscape as the OS''s per-pose presentation. The owner''s ruling cancels Option A''s branch and pins the tall hold, the closed pose, other devices and phones; it does not order the OS to letterbox the landscape hold, and manufacturing that letterbox would be the hack the ruling bans. Committed: the tall hold must meet the acceptance criteria; the landscape hold''s reading is recorded on the row either way. If the tall canvas proves reachable only through the Duo-keyed all-four mask row 271 carried - the cancelled gate''s bytes - the row moves to Blocked with that evidence instead of landing it.
- 2026-10-20 D5 The rotation instrument is agent-device''s orientation command (orientation <portrait|portrait-upside-down|landscape-left|landscape-right>, documented "Set device orientation on iOS and Android") beside fold for the poses; row 271''s lane had no rotation command, which is why the tall hold read as beyond its instruments. Device Hub''s rotate buttons (named in Tech Talks 111461) are the GUI fallback. The Duo simulator stays row 271''s until its lane returns.
- 2026-10-20 D6 No JS change. The tall window delivers real insets into useSafeAreaInsets; honoredTopInset keeps its 2.1.24 clamp (a tall-hold reading above 62 is transcribed on the row, and any clamp change stays behind an owner order - row 271''s Decision 5 precedent); Screen.tsx''s 500pt centred column renders portrait top-down in the tall canvas unchanged. The one-centred-schedule design ruling stands; nothing is redesigned.
- 2026-10-20 D7 The live-pose limitation carries over, recorded not chased: a process launched while the Duo is closed keeps its letterboxed rendering after unfold until its next relaunch (row 271''s Step 2b closed both re-ask attempts as refused by the system). No SceneDelegate insertion lands in this row.

## Files

Read (evidence, no change):
- ios/Athan/AppDelegate.swift and ios/Athan/Info.plist in the main tree (the prebuild products; the class-close marker "}[newline][newline]class ReactNativeDelegate: ExpoReactNativeFactoryDelegate {" verified present this session; both orientation arrays portrait-only today).
- node_modules/expo/ios/Expo/ExpoAppDelegate.swift (the open supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor seam, row 271''s line 243 record) and node_modules/@expo/config-plugins'' Orientation behaviour.
- plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.js (the repo''s plist-pinning pattern) and, read from the lane/271 ref without merging any of it, plugins/duoLandscapeGate.js with its test (the cancelled pattern this row''s plugin adapts).
- Apple''s Duo material: developer.apple.com/iphone-duo/prepare/, the Tech Talks 111461 transcript, the WWDC26 session 278 transcript, the HIG page "Designing for iPhone Duo".
- Rows 264 and 271 for the measured geometry (951x669 inner, 466x678 closed outer, 469x669 letterbox); e2e/README.md Gotchas before any measurement.
- shared/insets.ts, app/Screen.tsx, shared/constants.ts SIZE (the clamp and the centred column).

Changed (Step 2 only, and only if Step 1 reads the tall hold letterboxed):
- plugins/duoTallCanvas.js (new): the plist maximum plus the keying-free portrait-pair AppDelegate gate; pure exported edit functions, idempotent, throwing when the marker moves.
- plugins/__tests__/duoTallCanvas.test.ts (new): written red first.
- app.json: "./plugins/duoTallCanvas" named in plugins directly after "./plugins/portraitOnlyIpad".
- package.json and app.json version bump (patch) on every commit; board/snapshot/board.sql regenerated per commit; scripts/version-collisions-allowlist.json only when a cross-lane version collision lands.

## Design

The Duo''s inner display presents per pose and ignores the app''s orientation preference (D1). The app opts into the edge-to-edge treatment by declaring the four-orientation maximum in the plist; the app delegate then returns the portrait pair for every window, which pins every phone and the closed outer display to portrait - Apple documents the delegate method as the per-window authority intersected at every rotation decision, and row 271 measured phone-class rotation refusals in both directions under exactly this machinery. On the inner display the OS chooses: the tall hold presents the tall canvas carrying the app''s existing portrait layout, and whatever the landscape hold presents is transcribed (D4). No JS learns the pose, no layout value moves, and the closed pose, every phone and every Android device keep today''s behaviour byte-for-byte.

The invariant: on every screen except the Duo''s inner display the app''s effective orientation set equals today''s portrait-only plist exactly; on the inner display nothing is keyed, branched or requested - the OS''s per-pose presentation is the whole of Option B.

Outcome branches, exact:
- Step 1 reads the tall hold spanning edge to edge on today''s build: Option B is already the OS''s presentation of the shipped app. Step 2 and Step 3 each close with a one-line record naming Step 1''s reading, the branch carries no code, and the row proceeds to its audit.
- Step 1 reads the tall hold letterboxed: Step 2 lands the plugin and Step 3 re-measures. A gated build whose tall hold spans tall proceeds to the audit. A gated build whose tall hold still letterboxes, or presents landscape only, is Apple''s OS refusing the portrait-tall presentation: the row moves to Blocked with Waiting on naming the refusal, the docs'' quotes and the transcribed readings already on the row, and the lane returns BLOCKED with no workaround attempted.

Alternatives rejected:
- Option A''s Duo-keyed landscape gate: cancelled by the owner''s final ruling, never merges.
- requestGeometryUpdate or UIDevice setValue asks: refused or unsupported, and the hack the owner banned.
- expo-screen-orientation lockAsync: the system may refuse it on iOS 27, it lands asynchronously after launch leaving the plist maximum unguarded, and it is a new dependency behind the ask-first rule (row 271''s Decision 1).
- A SceneDelegate insertion re-earning spanning on live unfold: closed impossible by row 271''s Step 2b, both attempts refused by the system (D7).

## Steps

1. Step 1: the tall hold produced and today''s build read in it (device, 3 points). Runs alone in a fresh executor session, dispatched by the lane only after row 271''s lane has returned, its worktree is gone and the lead''s cancellation stands on row 271. Read e2e/README.md''s Gotchas first. Version bump (patch), then build this branch onto the iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1): npx expo run:ios -d SIMULATOR_UDID --configuration Release. Establish the instrument: fold open, then orientation landscape-left and landscape-right - the canvas class the OS reports for each hold (the tall 669x951 class expected; a refusal to rotate is itself the reading and names the Blocked branch). Then read today''s build: launched in the tall hold, the window class, the panel-edge pixels against the content bounds (letterbox verdict) and the delivered top and bottom insets; rotated live from the landscape hold into the tall hold under one pid - window class and letterbox verdict; the closed-pose control - portrait, today''s 466x678 class. Screenshots of the tall hold and the landscape-hold control attached to this row from outside the worktree, never committed; SIMULATOR_UDID placeholder in every committed record. One commit: version bump, snapshot, transcribed readings on this row.
2. Step 2: the orientation opt-in lands (code, 2 points). Lands only if Step 1 read the tall hold letterboxed; otherwise it closes with the one-line record naming Step 1''s spanning reading. Write plugins/__tests__/duoTallCanvas.test.ts first and watch it fail (red): editAppDelegate inserts the gate into a fixture AppDelegate.swift exactly once (idempotent second run), throws when the marker is absent, leaves ReactNativeDelegate untouched; the plist edit sets the four orientations; the exported Swift gate carries the literal portrait-pair mask and no panel-class constant; app.json names the plugin and keeps orientation "portrait"; the plugin survives every app.config.ts resolution. Land plugins/duoTallCanvas.js and the app.json entry (green); run the mutation pass on the edit''s guard arms. Version bump first (patch), then prebuild ios in the worktree (npx expo prebuild -p ios) and read the generated ios/Athan/AppDelegate.swift and Info.plist: the gate present inside class AppDelegate, the iPhone array carrying all four, the ~ipad array still portrait-only. yarn validate green. One commit.
3. Step 3: the tall canvas verified on the gated build (device, 3 points). Runs only if Step 2 landed code; otherwise it closes with the one-line record. Alone in a fresh executor session. Version bump (patch), rebuild onto the Duo simulator, then measure: open pose, tall hold - the window spans 669x951, the gradient edge to edge with no pure-black bars, the countdown and schedule centred in the 500pt column rendering top-down across both halves, insets transcribed; open pose, landscape hold - whatever the OS presents, transcribed with its window class; closed pose - a rotation attempt refused, portrait, today''s class; an open-closed-open round trip and a rotate round trip, each under one pid; phone-class simulator (iPhone 18 Pro class, iOS 27.0) - rotation attempts in both directions refused. Screenshots of the tall hold attached to this row, never committed. The Blocked branch fires here on a refused tall canvas (Design). One commit.
4. Step 4: the row''s records closed (docs, 1 point). Read the branch diff and record that it names exactly the plugin, its test, app.json, the version files and board/snapshot/board.sql - or, on the zero-code branch, nothing but the version files and the snapshot; tick the subtasks; regenerate the snapshot. One commit.

Points: 3 + 2 + 3 + 1 = 9.

## Risks

- The OS presents the inner display landscape only and rotation is refused or ineffective: the tall hold never materialises. Shows as Step 1''s instrument reading; the row moves to Blocked with the evidence - the branch the owner pre-ruled.
- The plist maximum letterboxes the tall hold anyway, the compat treatment keyed on fixed orientation rather than pose: Step 3 reads it; Blocked branch.
- The tall canvas proves reachable only through the Duo-keyed all-four mask row 271 carried: Step 3 reads the keying-free gate letterboxing while the keyed shape spanned; Blocked per D4 rather than landing the cancelled keying.
- The landscape hold presents spanning landscape once the plist widens, a rendering the owner cancelled as the Duo''s presentation: recorded on the row per D4; a ruling to change it lands in a new row.
- A live rotate or unfold keeps the launch-pose rendering until relaunch: row 271''s measured UIKit behaviour, carried as a recorded limitation (D7) and re-verified in Step 3''s round trips.
- An open-pose top inset above 62: the 2.1.24 clamp eats it; transcribed on the row per D6, no code change without an owner order.
- A future SDK moves the AppDelegate template: the marker edit throws at prebuild, never silently no-ops, and the unit tests pin the marker against the current template shape.
- Cross-lane version collisions at merge: uat is read fresh at merge time, the next free version is taken, and any interleaving is recorded in the ticket''s audit comment and allowlisted only when the gate requires it.

## Proof

- The tall canvas renders portrait top-down, edge to edge, no letterbox: Step 1''s or Step 3''s transcribed window class and panel-edge readings on this row, with the attached screenshot.
- The closed pose stays portrait, no other device rotates, phones unchanged: Step 1''s or Step 3''s transcribed refusals and window classes; on the zero-code branch, Step 4''s record that the branch diff names no code; yarn validate green at every step commit.
- Option A never merges: Step 4''s branch-diff record naming no lane/271 content; lane/271 itself is never merged by this lane and its worktree is deleted by its own lane.
- A refusal, if it comes, is documented: the Blocked move''s Waiting on line, the docs'' quotes carried in this plan''s D1, and the transcribed simulator readings on the row.
