- Dim solids hold under 4.5:1 at the gradient''s bright end (names 1.38:1) — that equals today''s local contrast; dims are de-emphasis and the primary ink holds 9.5:1 or better everywhere.
- A missed cell in the disabled matrix leaves one element''s off-state read wrong. What shows it: the Alert sheet with the athan off and with each reminder off; the matrix table names every cell.
- The jest suite keeps opacity assertions Step 1 knowingly leaves red until Step 2; the prototype commits on tsc and biome alone, per the owner''s order.
- The QiblaSubtitle colour crossfade covers the waiting line with opaque ready ink mid-fade instead of blending two translucent lines: no compounding (the point), a slightly different 150 ms.
- The entrance-fade ruling (transitions that pass between settled states stay view compositing) is the plan''s own line, not the owner''s; the owner''s Step 4 read is its verdict.

## Proof

- Step 1: `npx tsc --noEmit` and `yarn biome check` green on lane/294; `git grep -n "opacity" -- components app | grep -v __tests__` returns only the ruled-out list; the commit carries the triple version bump.
- Step 2: `yarn validate` green; planting `<Text style={{ opacity: 0.5 }}>` turns the sweep test red.
- Step 3: the same grep clean for `Prayer.tsx`, `Ago.tsx`, `OverlayInfoBox.tsx`; `useDerivedOpacity` has no consumer whose style lands on text.
- Step 4: the owner''s read, recorded in this ticket.
- Step 5: the merge commit on uat and `board/snapshot.sh` in the close commit.


## Prior art (researched 2026-10-21, owner-ordered)

The defect is a recognised, cross-platform bug class, and the wider world has ruled on it:

- **Android/AOSP itself carries our exact bug**: issue 565711357 (filed 2026-09-24, Assigned, P2) - "partially transparent Arabic text shows visible seams, brighter patches, or darker patches at cursive joins between connected glyphs... observed in multiple Android applications". The expected result there equals this ticket''s bar: uniform opacity across the whole shaped text run. No fix has shipped.
- **The W3C ruled in 2020**: w3c/csswg-drafts#5139 led to CSS Color 4''s Transparency section being amended (2020-07-27) to require opacity on the whole element, never per glyph - with Arabic text as the worked example. Per-glyph translucency that exposes cursive joins is non-conformant behaviour by spec.
- **Engines today**: WebKit composes Arabic runs as one layer and shows no seam (bug 257746 - correct for Arabic, Devanagari, Syriac, N''Ko; still broken for Adlam, Mongolian, Hanifi Rohingya, Mandaic). Chromium (issue 40819119, duplicate of 41284366, P2) and Gecko still compound. This is why iOS never showed us the seam while the 3T did.
- **The two known redresses**: (1) group/layer compositing - force the engine to flatten the text run into a layer, then alpha the layer (the web workaround in Mugo''s write-up; what Apple''s stack does natively). On our Android stack this is unavailable until AOSP fixes the text stack - wrapper opacity still compounds, which our own B-site sweep measured. (2) Solid pre-blended ink - dim carried by opaque colour tokens. This is what per-glyph platforms ship: Android''s own theme text colours (textColorHint, textColorTertiary) are opaque values, and Material''s "38% disabled text" ships as pre-flattened colour. Apple can afford translucent label colours only because Core Text flattens the run.

Conclusion: with the owner''s no-platform-split order, solid ink everywhere is the one road that works on both stacks, which is what this plan builds. Upstream fixes (AOSP, Chromium) are worth watching but none is shippable today.
